WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.450
LOW: I'm Francis Low.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.030
And let me welcome
you here tonight.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.640
And let me start by
calling on Mr. Greg

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.805
Mitchell of the Student
Disarmament Study Group,

00:00:24.805 --> 00:00:26.430
who would like to
make an announcement.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.603
Mr. Mitchell--

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.320
MITCHELL: On behalf of the
MIT Students Disarmament Study

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.180
Group, I'd like to
bring to your attention

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.520
a project we've been
working on for quite a

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.280
while is within hours
of reaching fruition.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.530
If you're interested in
speaking with your congressman

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.860
about arms race issues,
now's your chance.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.002
Massachusetts students,
staff, and faculty

00:00:50.002 --> 00:00:52.210
will be joining with thousands
from across the nation

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.840
tomorrow for a day of
lobbying in Washington, DC.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.510
Buses will be leaving MIT
at 11:00 PM this evening.

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:02.360
After a day at
the capital, we'll

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.873
arrive back in Boston
early Friday morning.

00:01:04.873 --> 00:01:07.572
The cost us $30.

00:01:07.572 --> 00:01:09.030
All the appointments
have been made

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.010
with senators, and
representatives,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.980
and their aides for all
the states in our nation.

00:01:16.332 --> 00:01:17.790
If you're interested,
please see us

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.915
right outside the lecture
hall after this evening's

00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:20.965
activities.

00:01:20.965 --> 00:01:22.590
And after that,
there'll be a reception

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:28.130
for Massachusetts lobbyists in
room 407 of the Student Center.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:29.550
The trip should
be quite exciting

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.360
and a worthwhile
experience for all.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.988
So thank you.

00:01:32.988 --> 00:01:36.474
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.010
LOW: Let me welcome you again.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.050
My name hasn't changed.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.800
So I welcome you to this
year's Compton Lecture.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.790
Tonight we mark the 40th
year since the explosion

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.280
of the first nuclear device
in the New Mexican desert.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:06.120
11 present and
retired MIT professors

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.030
were involved in that
effort at Los Alamos

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.470
that culminated in that event.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:20.240
Hence, the title of today's
set of talks and things

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.750
happening 40 years after
Los Alamos MIT and the bomb.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.560
Eight of those MIT faculty
members are here tonight.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.870
I would also hope
to see in that title

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:42.410
a reflection of the commitment
of so many members of my team's

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:48.470
community to doing something
about the situation in which we

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.310
in the world find ourselves.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:55.910
These Los Alamos veterans
who are here tonight

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.570
have participated, in
lesser or greater degree,

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:03.620
many with great devotion
in that activity.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.900
They have worked
throughout these 40 years

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.360
to contain the effects of
their terrible invention.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.960
Their work made war obsolete,
but unfortunately it

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.380
did not make war impossible.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.050
The existence of nuclear weapons
poses the greatest threat

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.110
since Nemesis to the
survival of life on Earth.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.580
And if not life
on Earth, at least

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.830
the survival of
our civilization,

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.140
civilization as we know it.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:33.680
I hope that the
meeting tonight will

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.620
help us think fruitfully and
usefully about what we can do

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.490
about these terrible questions.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.726
I have to tell you--

00:03:45.726 --> 00:03:49.900
I'm sorry to have to tell
you that Joe Wiesner will

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.530
be unable to join us.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.200
Mrs. Wiesner is in the hospital.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.790
It's not life threatening.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.767
But she is confined-- she
is for the moment confined,

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:02.350
leaving the hospital
today in Florida.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:03.555
And he had to be with her.

00:04:03.555 --> 00:04:07.720
And he sends his best
wishes and regrets.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.080
So we will hear tonight from the
two others scheduled speakers.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.490
After their talk,
we are also honored

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:18.470
to have with us
tonight Mr. Bajpai, who

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.250
is the ambassador from
India to Washington.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.390
After their talk, I will
ask him for a few comments.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:32.120
And after that, we will open the
meeting to general discussion.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.960
The two speakers are
both old friends of mine

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.600
and of many of us here.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.418
I can't imagine two
different people.

00:04:44.418 --> 00:04:46.460
But when I think of how
I should introduce them--

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:50.210
I have not been given
a prepared vita--

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.850
I mean, actually their vitas are
so long and so distinguished,

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.890
that one couldn't do
much with it anyway.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.640
But as I thought about what
I want to say about them,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.510
the same things come
to mind, although they

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:05.820
are extraordinarily
different people,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:06.890
They are both physicists.

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:09.360
They are both
distinguished physicists.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.210
They are MIT
Institute professors.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:23.140
They are incredibly
learned, humane, humanist,

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.679
two wonderful people.

00:05:25.679 --> 00:05:26.645
And--

00:05:26.645 --> 00:05:29.543
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:39.000
And, of course,
after they've talked,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.560
you'll tell the difference.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.050
And I have no titles.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:47.040
They'll give you
their own titles.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.070
The first then is
Victor Weisskopf--

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.870
Viki--

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:57.774
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:06.160
WEISSKOPF: My friends, I
don't have a special title.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:15.270
40 years after, thoughts
inspired by those 40 years.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:21.390
40 years ago, in the early
morning hours of July 16,

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:24.660
a truck driver in
New Mexico reported

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:29.280
that he saw the sun
about to rise at 4:00 AM.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.320
But then the sun decided
it was too early,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.070
and went back again, and
came up an hour earlier--

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.380
an hour later.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.150
And that was, of course,
the historic event

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:45.270
of the first man-made atomic
explosion, which some of you

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.400
saw this afternoon in the movie.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:54.570
I have witnessed this event,
actually, roughly at the same--

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.140
actually, exactly at
the same place as Phil.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:01.740
I remember we both
were sitting together.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.140
The description we
got from the army

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.630
was that we should lie
down with our stomachs--

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:10.573
on our stomachs, with our
heads away from the explosion.

00:07:10.573 --> 00:07:12.240
Of course, we wouldn't
dream to do that,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.520
because we wanted to
see what's going on.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.800
So after the generals who
were there had put themselves

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.171
into that position--

00:07:21.171 --> 00:07:23.526
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:33.840
Phil and I, and many others
there, took our dark glasses--

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.230
we figured out
that the intensity

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:41.180
should be roughly 20 midday
suns at that distance,

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.380
and looked at it, and tried to
measure the size to estimate

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.330
the efficiency and so on.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:52.770
It was, indeed, an
incredible sight

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:59.150
and an awesome experience, which
hardly is desirable in words.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:05.030
Imagine the landscape lit up 20
times stronger than sunlight.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:09.800
And even at that distance, we
felt an intense blast of heat

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:15.230
and a roaring thunder after
three quarters of a minute.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:20.256
And we saw this steadily
ascending fireball, made

00:08:20.256 --> 00:08:26.540
a brilliant hemisphere,
up into a sphere,

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.440
becoming slowly
white, yellow, orange,

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.900
surrounded by this blue halo.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:38.350
And then finally, assuming
the well-known mushroom shape.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.010
And then the sun
actually went up

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:49.420
and illuminated the whole
thing with the red sunlight.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:53.530
Suddenly a credible impression
that filled all of us

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:58.660
with awe, with pride
of unique achievement,

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.980
and suddenly with misgivings
about the future of mankind.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:09.000
A few weeks after
that trial, two bombs

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.020
were thrown over
two cities in Japan.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.330
Whatever one may
think about the wisdom

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.630
of the American
use of the bombs,

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.540
it did end the murderous war.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.500
And most probably saved
many hundreds of thousands,

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:29.130
if not millions of
people, on both sides.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:33.284
It may have saved many
more than it has destroyed.

00:09:33.284 --> 00:09:35.544
We meant so well.

00:09:35.544 --> 00:09:37.860
And we served our country.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.180
And we thought that
such powerful weapons

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:44.150
would make wars impossible.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:47.300
And we hoped very much
that this whole thing

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:52.250
would lead to an international
administration of every thing

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:56.556
atomic or nuclear
power in weapons.

00:09:56.556 --> 00:10:00.100
And then it would be
an end of the old--

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:05.230
the age-old human custom of
organized mutual mass murder.

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:12.580
We went out and gave talks
to civic organizations,

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:16.990
to the people in Santa Fe,
and other cities nearby.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:22.300
And I remember I was quoting
John Donne at that time--

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:26.080
no man is an island
entirely for himself.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:31.490
Every man is a piece of the
continent, a part of the main.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:35.980
Never ask for whom the bell
tolls, it tolls for thee.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:38.800
And what we meant is that
this will bring, finally,

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.100
mankind together.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.440
Well, we were naive and wrong.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.330
We should have known better.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.160
True enough, the
existence of the bomb

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:51.220
has kept peace for the
last 40 years, at least,

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:54.170
between the big powers.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:58.100
This is an unusually
long period.

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.410
But these powers have
found only one way

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.150
to maintain the
state of affairs,

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:05.360
to indulge in an
ever-increasing arms race.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.540
More and more weapons were made.

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.340
And at this moment
in history, I do

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:13.680
believe that we are
on a collision course.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.090
The nuclear armaments
go ahead, accelerated.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.880
New types of weapons
are introduced.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.290
Technical innovations that make
the weapons more efficient,

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:30.740
but any kind of arms control
more difficult. [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.640
the consequences of a potential
military conflict become

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:47.500
increasingly more [INAUDIBLE]
with a total explosive power

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.730
6,000 times more
than all the bombs

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:54.590
that were exploded
during the World War I.

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:59.850
Now this, to my mind, when only
a 50th perhaps is necessary,

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:08.900
complete [INAUDIBLE] this case
of collective mental disease

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:11.525
that has gripped humanity.

00:12:11.525 --> 00:12:15.230
And I think it will be seen
as such in 30-40 years,

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.520
if we survive.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:22.010
And all that came from us,
from our work at Los Alamos

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.590
and other of these laboratories.

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:29.500
It came from our brain
child, from our achievements

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:30.480
40 years ago.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:36.500
Many statesmen and a
good part of the public

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.770
is today aware of the
fact that the actual use

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:42.770
of even a small part of all
this would be the greatest

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:44.520
catastrophe of mankind.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.620
But being aware is not enough.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:55.140
We must recognize why
is it that we go on

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:59.840
and increase the probability
of the danger over nuclear war.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:03.320
Why must that threat, which
supposedly keeps peace and did

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.840
keep peace, why must this threat
be multiplied all the time

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.230
by a steadily increasing factor?

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:12.000
Why cannot people in governments
get their heads together

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.650
and recognize--

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:16.380
and say, enough is
enough and recognize

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.200
the danger of all this?

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.450
I believe that one
of the main reasons

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:25.420
is that there are
many kinds of fear.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:28.470
There is, for example, the
fear of a first strike.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:38.360
Both sides are afraid that
the other side [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.210
and particularly the land based
missiles before retaliation.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:45.320
Now that fear is in many
ways more irrational,

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:49.400
because the US has more
than half of its missiles

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:53.350
in submarines, which are,
at least for a good time,

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:57.650
impossible to exactly locate,
so they cannot be destroyed,

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:58.830
most of them, at least.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.950
And the Russians
have about a quarter

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:04.260
of their missiles in submarines.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:08.970
And given the destructive
power, if even one submarine--

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:12.230
it is plain that a
first strike just

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:14.270
could not disarm an adversary.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:20.200
Another fear is the fear to
be weaker than the other side.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:24.150
See, up to the mid
-70s, the United States

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:26.170
had considerably more
weapons, nuclear weapons,

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:28.580
than the Soviet Union.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:30.830
Although the latter
had even at that time

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:34.545
enough weapons to destroy us.

00:14:34.545 --> 00:14:41.055
But since then, the Soviet Union
has reached a sort of equality

00:14:41.055 --> 00:14:47.500
with us and that has people
very uneasy, to say the least.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:52.420
But let me say, this increase
of the Russian nuclear weapons,

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:55.680
which happened in the second
part of the '70s and beginning

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:04.160
of the '80s is has been preceded
by a very strong increase

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:08.298
of the United States
from about 2,000 to 8,000

00:15:08.298 --> 00:15:09.729
at strategic points.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:18.350
So we are now roughly
at the same level.

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:21.360
Ironically, just that
approximate parity

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.896
adds a lot to the
present difficulties

00:15:23.896 --> 00:15:25.630
and to the arms race.

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:26.130
Why?

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:27.780
Because you never know.

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:30.630
And there's a lot of secrecy,
particularly on the other side.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:37.982
And therefore, you [INAUDIBLE]
worst, and to think

00:15:37.982 --> 00:15:39.190
there are more weapons there.

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:43.430
And therefore, has to build
more and build them up.

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:45.520
This is one of the
driving forces, of course,

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:46.750
of the arms race.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:50.020
The second-- the
third fear, actually,

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:53.250
is of being out distanced--

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.970
out distanced in a
certain type of weapon.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:00.430
For example, the Russians
have actually more land based

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.480
and we have more
submarine weapons.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:05.710
So we think we have to have
the same number of land based

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:06.580
missiles.

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:08.965
And the Russians have those
big land based missiles.

00:16:08.965 --> 00:16:12.130
And that is one reason why
people want to build the MX.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.530
So actually, we're
going to build the MX.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:18.340
And the Soviet Union, of
course, on the other side,

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:20.920
is going to build
submarines, because they

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:22.600
have fewer than we are.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.580
This is, again, and
mechanism for escalation.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.900
A third fear-- or this
is now the fourth fear,

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:35.760
comes from a perceived or
an actual aggressive policy

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.760
of the opponent.

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.890
Both sides believe
that the other side

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:43.540
is bent on aggression,
whereas they, themselves,

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:45.886
act purely defensively.

00:16:45.886 --> 00:16:48.870
Of course, the US
definitely says

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:50.580
that what happened
in Afghanistan

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:55.110
is an aggressive act,
or in Southeast Asia.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.830
But this is seen by the other
side as a defense effect,

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:03.060
to ensure the security of their
borders, where in Vietnam,

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:05.839
the Russians to establish
a counterbalance

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:07.829
to the influence of China.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:14.579
Conversely, the US has a
lot of defensive actions

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:16.740
to prevent the spread
of communist influence,

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.210
for example in Central
America, in the Middle East.

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:20.790
And these are
perceived, of course,

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:22.260
by the Soviet
Union as an attempt

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:25.920
to build up Western
influence wherever possible.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:29.030
And the recent Star War plans
is a particular good example.

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.530
It is perceived by
the Soviet Union

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.020
as a preparation
for first strike,

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.570
because then we can
have a first strike

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:40.320
and at least the
consequences of retaliation

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:45.480
will be diminished, so that you
can perhaps supposedly support

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:49.320
them, which I don't believe,
but perhaps they believe.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:51.840
So each military
and political action

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.830
abroad can be interpreted
as aggressive or defensive

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:56.490
by one side or the other.

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:58.800
And during a political
crisis, of course,

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:04.610
such misrepresentations
are extremely dangerous.

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:06.410
The lack of understanding
comes, of course,

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:09.350
from a very completely
different system, social system,

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:11.200
political system.

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:14.290
We are afraid of the
authoritarian regime of world

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:19.090
communism, the lack of
elementary human rights

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:22.420
on the other side, the
treatment of minorities,

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:24.620
the suppression of
freedom in the satellites

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.425
and the homeland.

00:18:26.425 --> 00:18:29.650
That arouses fears
in the United States.

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:34.480
And vise-versa, the ideology
of capitalist free enterprise,

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:38.920
the human rights activities,
the virulent anti-communism,

00:18:38.920 --> 00:18:42.970
strong support of right wing
dictators, and lately the talk

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:47.680
about liberating the world
from the evils of communism,

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:50.300
necessarily arouses fear
in the Soviet Union,

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:53.360
and not only in the
leading circles.

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:56.820
Now, in order to
understand a little more,

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:00.290
I think that's a very
important thing, the fears

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:04.300
and the subsequent
actions of our adversary,

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:07.540
you must consider the
present situation.

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.470
Let me express it this way.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:13.250
There are four large
power complexes--

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:18.170
China, the Soviet Union,
Western Europe, United States.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:21.575
Three of them have one enemy--

00:19:21.575 --> 00:19:25.040
or let me adversary--
the Soviet Union.

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:28.370
One of them, the Soviet Union,
has four-- namely the three

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:30.290
others and its own population.

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:32.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:35.650
Now, it's no laughing
matter for them.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:39.622
And this explains
a lot of actions.

00:19:39.622 --> 00:19:41.580
For example, one of the
reasons-- certainly one

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:44.460
of the reasons of the
large conventional forces

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:49.370
in Soviet Union is the fear
of revolts in Eastern Europe.

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:54.500
The overly large number
of Soviet SS 20 missiles

00:19:54.500 --> 00:20:00.260
is probably meant to deter
the Western aid for revolts,

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:03.860
possible revolts
in Eastern Europe,

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:08.340
and to support incipient
anti-Soviet governments.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:10.040
Now, the fact that
the West never

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.310
has supported such
movements does not

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:17.140
allay their fears that they
may do it in the future.

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:22.350
Now, these fears are important,
but they are not the only cause

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:23.520
of the armaments race.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:29.820
I am coming now to another
very dangerous and important--

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:32.640
more and more important
cause of the armaments race.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.760
And that is the
increasing momentum

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:37.680
of the military machine.

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.040
Years of intense developments,
and buildup of weapons,

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.410
of means of delivery,
of new methods

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:47.250
to improve the accuracy, they've
established and interwoven

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:53.550
system of vested interests, of
ambitious and dedicated groups

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:58.770
unwilling to give up their
successful activities, not

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:01.230
only, or maybe not
even very much,

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:02.758
because of financial interests.

00:21:02.758 --> 00:21:04.300
Although, I'm sure
this plays a role.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.870
But also, because of
a natural commitment

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:09.990
to pursue a difficult
task, just when

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:13.290
it's about to yield
more and better results.

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:16.920
No doubt most participants
believe that they will--

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:20.730
that what they do will enhance
the security of the United

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:23.280
States or the Soviet Union.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:27.090
But it is probably not so.

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:31.670
How can an armaments
race increase security?

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:33.900
I remember this very
well from personal

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:35.022
experience in Los Alamos.

00:21:35.022 --> 00:21:37.230
I think this afternoon there
was a little discussion,

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:38.910
but I will still mention it now.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.760
Namely, in Los Alamos, we all--

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:45.120
most of us, like myself,
and many of my friends,

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.880
went to Los Alamos only
because we were afraid

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:50.490
that Hitler will make the bomb.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:53.970
Still, when the bomb--

00:21:53.970 --> 00:22:01.090
when Hitler was defeated, just
about 40 years ago in May,

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:03.880
there wasn't even
discussion of quitting.

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:06.340
We just worked on
because we had also

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:10.240
this technological
momentum, that--

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:14.230
Oppenheimer uses
this uncanny term,

00:22:14.230 --> 00:22:18.750
a technically sweet project.

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:19.730
That's the danger.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:22.710
Of course, the same
danger of this momentum

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:25.065
is also in our
preparations on both sides,

00:22:25.065 --> 00:22:29.680
a preparation to engage
in a nuclear war.

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.840
Strategies and
tactics are developed.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:34.510
An escalating
search for military

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:37.830
targets to be hit in case.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:39.500
And the more such
targets are found,

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:43.330
the more bombs have to
be deployed to hit them.

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:46.360
Should the deterrents
fail, so goes the argument,

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:48.070
nuclear war may be
come and we must

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:51.980
be prepared to prevail
in a limited nuclear war.

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.770
But, of course, a nuclear
war very probably cannot be

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:55.640
limited.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:23:00.530
The losing side will
be forced to escalate.

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:04.550
Two archers facing one
another, saying the tighter I

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:09.790
draw the bow, the
safer I become.

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:12.160
Unfortunately, also
these preparations

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:15.380
are fueled by
their own momentum.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:17.710
The more developments going
on, the more possibilities

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:19.290
of attacks are found, as I said.

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:23.110
The more pressures
develop for deployment.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:28.810
And such preparations made
a certain sense in the past,

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:32.590
when war was really acceptable
as the last assault for which

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:34.270
we must be well prepared.

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:36.700
In my hometown, in Vienna,
there is a monument.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:41.980
And it says on the
monument, [SPEAKING LATIN]

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:44.860
For those who don't
know Latin, it

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:48.300
means when you wish for
peace, prepare for war.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:52.230
Today's that slogan can
no longer be applied.

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:57.490
To prepare for nuclear war is
to prepare for mutual suicide.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:03.510
So the arms race is the
product of irrational fears

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:06.240
felt by both contending powers.

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:09.480
And there is a
self-perpetuating mechanism

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:12.840
that creates its own momentum
and increases the fears,

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:17.050
and draws all the activities
into its whirlpool.

00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:21.280
And this is why the present
confrontation is so dangerous.

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:24.650
This is the first
part of my talk.

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:29.610
Now, I would like to
use the rest of the time

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:33.930
to those thoughts that must
come to people who are involved

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:36.420
with these questions.

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:37.930
What have we learned
now from what

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:39.920
happened in the last 40 years?

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:44.740
And how can we see
a hopeful future?

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.830
Which I do--

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:53.070
I don't believe-- at
least I feel in my bones

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:54.750
that we won't be all destroyed.

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:59.110
But only if we do
something about it.

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:06.010
Now, the last four decades have
taught us one important lesson.

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:09.250
Negotiations to reduce the
level of nuclear weapons

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:11.290
have very little
chance of success

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:15.970
in the prevailing atmosphere
of confrontation, distrust,

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:21.300
and increasingly offensive and
defensive weapon developments.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:23.480
To the contrary,
there is a danger

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:29.540
of the erosion of even
those few treaties that

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:35.890
did slow down the armaments
race, like SALT I and SALT II.

00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:38.200
Perhaps, I should
like to make a remark.

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:42.430
SALT I has an interesting
part, which many of you know,

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:45.310
namely the ABM
Treaty, which says

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:47.980
that you are not supposed
to defend yourself

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:50.420
against strategic missiles.

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:57.160
So quite so, you are
allowed to have one or two

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:58.630
establishments for that.

00:25:58.630 --> 00:26:00.940
Actually, the United States
has not used it at all

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:03.810
and the Russians
have one, Moscow.

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:09.040
Now, this was a very
difficult development.

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:12.190
And I remember at
that time, I was

00:26:12.190 --> 00:26:14.770
active in the Pugwash movement.

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:18.280
From '64-- that treaty was
concluded in the '70s--

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:19.120
'72.

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:24.790
From '64 on, we tried to talk
with the Russian scientists

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:28.990
to tell them that such defenses
are dangerous, because all they

00:26:28.990 --> 00:26:33.280
will do is to increase the
number of offensive warheads,

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:36.130
so that an equilibrium,
so-called equilibrium is

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:37.750
established.

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.380
It was difficult to
convince the Russians.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.000
Indeed, the Russian scientists
used the same arguments

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:46.930
which you hear today from
our government-- defense

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:51.970
obviously is good, how
can defense be bad?

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:53.980
We must defend ourselves.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:58.960
And it took us many years,
but we succeeded, not

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:02.530
only to convince the scientists,
our government, at that time,

00:27:02.530 --> 00:27:04.900
was also of that opinion.

00:27:04.900 --> 00:27:08.530
And the scientists that
came to Pugwash, of course,

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:11.520
had very strong connections
with their government,

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:15.010
or they wouldn't be allowed
to come out to the West.

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:18.820
And they were able to convince
their government really

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:24.010
to sign that treaty,
which to some extent

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:27.190
has reduced the speed
of the armaments race,

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:28.570
though it didn't turn it around.

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:31.630
But the interesting thing,
I tell this only because

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:34.110
of the application of today.

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:37.070
Today the situation
seems to be reversed.

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:39.490
The same group of scientists
that convinced, at that time

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:43.120
the Russian scientists, that
defense, that kind of defense

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:47.380
against missiles will only
increase the armaments race,

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:50.230
we are not successful in
convincing our government,

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.450
our present government of this.

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.140
And vise versa,
the Russians now,

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:57.970
who we had a hard time
to convince, now say

00:27:57.970 --> 00:28:02.283
exactly that idea and
repeat it, and probably

00:28:02.283 --> 00:28:04.900
with good justification.

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:08.830
Well, this is just
to say that there

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:15.640
were certain efforts already
before, during these 40 years.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:18.070
But there is now a chance
that all these treaties--

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:22.480
I wrote it-- for example, the
Star War project, of course,

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:26.230
would, if it actually goes into
the testing and development

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:29.950
phase, would be a
breach of the ABF

00:28:29.950 --> 00:28:37.770
Treaty, which has saved us so
far from too much development.

00:28:37.770 --> 00:28:45.360
Is there a chance that we can
arrive at a different attitude?

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:46.690
Evidently, we must.

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:48.180
There are compelling reasons.

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:52.690
And that is sort of
clear to everybody.

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:59.750
And their main point is
that nuclear weapons must

00:28:59.750 --> 00:29:02.330
be regarded not
as weapons of war,

00:29:02.330 --> 00:29:06.530
but their only purpose
is to deter their use

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:08.330
by the other side.

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:11.000
And for this, of course, we
have much fewer than we have

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:12.440
at present.

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:17.030
Now, how can we
change those causes

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:18.980
which I've analyzed
before, limiting

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:22.995
mutual fears and the driving
forces of increasing armament

00:29:22.995 --> 00:29:23.495
efforts?

00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:32.145
Well, we must be aware
that the present situation,

00:29:32.145 --> 00:29:34.760
the present confrontational
of the situation,

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:40.170
inducing fear in the opponent
is against one's own interest.

00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:42.010
It only escalates
the armaments race.

00:29:44.818 --> 00:29:47.980
Fear, of course, is
not an objective fact,

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:49.570
like the number of warheads.

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:52.990
It arises from interpretation
or misinterpretations

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:55.360
of statements and actions
by the other side.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:04.390
Now, what could be ways to
reduce fears and distrust.

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:06.850
I think an
indispensable first step

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:09.440
is an awareness of the problem.

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:13.900
For example, every statement
and every action on both sides

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:18.880
should be examined with the
view as to how will it be

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:20.540
interpreted on the other side.

00:30:20.540 --> 00:30:24.670
Will it increase or reduce
the fear and distrust?

00:30:24.670 --> 00:30:28.000
Obviously, you can
easily see examples

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:29.680
that increase the fear.

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:32.380
The deployment of
MX, the deployment

00:30:32.380 --> 00:30:34.240
of the Trident II
submarines that

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:37.270
are accurate enough
to hit their missiles,

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:41.106
on our side, increase fear.

00:30:41.106 --> 00:30:44.290
The great number,
the heavy SS18s

00:30:44.290 --> 00:30:46.690
and the recent preparations
for deployments

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:52.680
of mobile missiles by the
Soviet Union increases the fear.

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:58.680
Of course, SS20 in Europe also.

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:03.220
Well, how can we deal
with those fears?

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:07.820
For example, how can we deal
with the fear of superiority?

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:11.690
Let's be sure, today both
sides, the East and the West

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:14.990
maintain that opponent
is militarily stronger.

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:17.180
You can read this
in our paper and you

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:19.910
can read it in their papers.

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:22.490
As remarked before,
it is not so much

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:26.870
the reality that counts,
but the perception.

00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:30.530
Therefore, we must not only
strive to maintain an offensive

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:34.980
and defensive parity, because
if we try to be superior,

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:36.660
we only accelerate
the arms race,

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:41.990
because evidently they cannot
allow us to be superior.

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:44.780
Perhaps I should search
your one little remark.

00:31:44.780 --> 00:31:47.750
One could say, aha, we're are
now technologically so far

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:51.020
ahead that we can become
superior and they cannot follow

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:51.690
us.

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:55.310
That would be the most dangerous
situation, because then they

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:57.730
would become--

00:31:57.730 --> 00:32:02.170
they would fear that we will use
our superiority for blackmail,

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:04.610
that they have to give
in here and there.

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:06.110
That's probably
also the rationale

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:11.047
of those people who are
in favor of superiority.

00:32:11.047 --> 00:32:12.380
But then they become irrational.

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:15.860
And governments become
very easily irrational.

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:19.190
And they will put
on the wall and they

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:25.520
will react in some way,
which induces a war.

00:32:25.520 --> 00:32:31.960
Now, therefore, we must be sure
that both the East and the West

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:37.030
have also the perception
of being on parity.

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:39.850
That means one of the
most important things

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:43.360
is to have some
negotiations that

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:52.010
have the aim to make it clear,
to discuss how is parity meant.

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:55.140
We must put ourselves a
the little in their shoes

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:56.810
to understand it.

00:32:56.810 --> 00:32:57.960
And we rarely do this.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:00.090
I'll give you an example.

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:03.300
The present dispute over the
number of intermediate range

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.460
missiles in Europe.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:10.050
The Soviets-- well,
we say we have

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:12.720
to put all these missiles
into European countries

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:17.870
because they have 10 times more
missiles, intermediate missiles

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.920
then we have, the SS20.

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:24.580
But of course, the Soviets
count the French and British

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:30.010
missiles, and find then that
both sides are about equal.

00:33:30.010 --> 00:33:31.880
Now, we leave them out.

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:33.670
Imagine, for example--
now here, one

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:36.640
has to use the mirror
argument and put oneself

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:37.640
in the other's position.

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:41.410
Imagine, for example,
if they would not

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:45.040
count missiles they have
put into Hungary, or Poland,

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:48.310
or Cuba as missiles against us.

00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:55.000
I mean, one has to have the
most primitive understanding

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:57.630
for the problem.

00:33:57.630 --> 00:34:02.160
So there should be bilateral
discussion, or maybe

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:04.680
multilateral discussions,
because the Allied countries

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:08.230
on both sides are very
much interested in this.

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:10.210
They don't want
to be unprotected

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:12.719
witness in the
defensive build up

00:34:12.719 --> 00:34:16.400
or launching pads in
an offensive build up.

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:23.989
Another thing is,
in order to get down

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:26.690
the fear of aggression.

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:29.310
And we must have a
better understanding

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:31.820
of their motivations
of their actions.

00:34:31.820 --> 00:34:35.550
Here, the national body
for crisis management

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:38.699
would, of course, be
just an important step.

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:42.000
That body would discuss
actual or potentially expected

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:44.969
political and military events.

00:34:44.969 --> 00:34:47.760
There already exist
such a bilateral body--

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:51.210
the standing
Consultative Commission,

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:56.010
that deals with supposed or real
infractions of the treaties,

00:34:56.010 --> 00:35:00.310
and it has been very useful
in the past to smooth--

00:35:00.310 --> 00:35:02.850
to get difficulties
out of the way.

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:09.685
Nowadays, when so-called
perceived infractions are right

00:35:09.685 --> 00:35:13.550
away widely publicized,
and so the Commission

00:35:13.550 --> 00:35:16.000
is no longer so effective.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.290
But there is a
reason that we should

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:21.290
have as similar, of
course, much harder task,

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:24.960
of international
monitor, or maybe later

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:32.420
on, multilateral body that
discusses possible crisis.

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:35.150
Now, there are obviously
military measures that

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:38.680
could be taken to reduce fears.

00:35:38.680 --> 00:35:41.850
We could, for example,
reduce our potential,

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:44.440
our strategic
potential by 10% or 20%

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:49.260
without weakening our position.

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:52.840
In particularly, we could reduce
first strike capabilities,

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:55.716
because we can't
use them anyway.

00:35:55.716 --> 00:36:01.420
And the [INAUDIBLE] of never
using nuclear weapons first,

00:36:01.420 --> 00:36:03.490
that would be also a
very important thing,

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:06.550
because a declaration
not to commit suicide

00:36:06.550 --> 00:36:08.320
shouldn't be so difficult.

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:11.440
Of course, the Western
Europeans always think,

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:16.910
and they reason, with
some justification,

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:19.330
that the nuclear
deterrence is important

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:21.670
against a conventional attack.

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:25.370
But this reasoning is based upon
a very questionable assumption,

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:30.800
namely that a suicide threat
is an effective deterrent.

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:36.660
So these are problems
which we have to attack.

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:38.620
Now, one trivial case.

00:36:38.620 --> 00:36:42.440
And that is that there are
8,000 small nuclear weapons,

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:47.360
so-called battlefield
weapons, around in Europe.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:49.810
And of course, both sides,
roughly this number.

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:53.010
And those make really no
sense at all on the countries.

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:54.310
They're extremely dangerous.

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:56.680
By the way, they
aren't that small.

00:36:56.680 --> 00:36:58.990
There are some of them that
are as big as the Hiroshima

00:36:58.990 --> 00:37:03.040
explosions, some of you
have seen this afternoon.

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:06.310
There is a real good chance
of a successful negotiation

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:09.160
on that, because actually,
the US has already reduced

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.280
them unilaterally somewhat.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:13.810
And it's obviously
an advantage to have

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:16.300
a higher threshold
against the outbreak

00:37:16.300 --> 00:37:20.480
of a nuclear conflagration.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:23.320
But these are all
smaller measures.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:25.560
What is necessary,
and certainly,

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:28.030
what the last 40
years have shown then

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:33.570
is to reduce tension by other
and more effective methods.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.250
We need a deeper
change of attitude

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:37.885
from this paranoid distrust.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:48.180
[INAUDIBLE] is--
the US and the SU--

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:52.440
I like the symmetry
in the abbreviations--

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:55.790
are not enemies,
they're adversaries.

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:59.680
They have one common enemy
and that is nuclear war.

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:02.080
And they have many
common interests, apart

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:07.698
from escaping mutual
nuclear annihilation.

00:38:07.698 --> 00:38:09.240
I'd like to talk a
little about that,

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:10.920
because this is important.

00:38:10.920 --> 00:38:14.730
Because to work together
on other dangers

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:18.690
is helpful for
mutual understanding.

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:22.650
The world is, for example,
threatened by the increase,

00:38:22.650 --> 00:38:25.470
the steady increase of
carbon dioxide, which

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:27.060
will change the climate.

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:29.740
It may melt the ice sheet.

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:33.442
We don't know much about it, but
coastal cities may disappear.

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:39.500
I saw a map the other day
of what could have happened,

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:42.260
which Florida is
gone, Holland is gone,

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:44.998
the whole coastal east is gone.

00:38:44.998 --> 00:38:48.750
They don't care about this.

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:51.210
Then they have the
chemical pollution,

00:38:51.210 --> 00:38:54.300
the atmosphere is
threatened, threatens animals

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:55.550
and vegetation.

00:38:55.550 --> 00:39:00.930
The acid rain,
and other things--

00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:03.450
the death of the forests,
which in Europe is

00:39:03.450 --> 00:39:05.430
a major catastrophe
and probably will

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:08.600
come to this country and the
Soviet Union pretty soon.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:12.930
We have so far protected because
our population density is not

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:14.430
as high.

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:17.610
Now here are things that need
scientific investigation, that

00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:20.400
need scientific collaboration,
that need big projects.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.300
But where are our
scientists, they

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:25.510
go into defense,
what we call defense.

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:29.910
They go into all this
weapons development

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:34.180
and the financial
support goes there,

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.550
whereas there are
many other dangers.

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:37.800
And I've only mentioned two.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:38.970
There are many more--

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:42.333
pollution of the
oceans, overpopulation,

00:39:42.333 --> 00:39:46.920
over-exploitation of soil,
famine, et cetera, et cetera.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:49.840
So all these dangers
threaten both superpowers.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:51.670
And here's a
wonderful opportunity

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:58.370
for cooperation, which will
reduce fear and distrust.

00:39:58.370 --> 00:40:04.000
Now and even the transfer
of scientific and technical

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:06.400
interests to other aims
and weapon [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:06.400 --> 00:40:10.000
would even help to counteract
the self-propelling momentum

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:13.270
of the armaments effort.

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:16.450
Maybe I'm too optimistic,
but one should try.

00:40:16.450 --> 00:40:19.980
So there are, of course--
there is, of course--

00:40:19.980 --> 00:40:22.267
well, then commerce.

00:40:22.267 --> 00:40:23.850
We should have much
more in commerces,

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:27.430
so that we are sort of
interdependent of each other.

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:31.400
That is a help and not a danger,
as it is often described.

00:40:31.400 --> 00:40:33.720
Europe is a little ahead of us.

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:36.690
They have the gas line
from Soviet Russia.

00:40:36.690 --> 00:40:38.245
They live a much more--

00:40:38.245 --> 00:40:40.370
they have much more commerce
with the Soviet Union,

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:41.940
et cetera.

00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:44.700
But of course, one
of the main obstacles

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:47.470
is always the question
of human rights.

00:40:47.470 --> 00:40:50.850
How can you collaborate
with a system that

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:53.630
treats its citizens that way?

00:40:53.630 --> 00:40:56.610
But I think that's just
the other way around.

00:40:56.610 --> 00:40:58.640
It is bad that they
treat their citizens,

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:00.660
but you must fight it,
and you must tell it,

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:02.130
and you must talk about it.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:06.630
But by not having any
collaboration and cooperation

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.690
with them, we make things
only worse, as we do.

00:41:09.690 --> 00:41:12.390
Since the so-called detente.

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:14.820
Detente, unfortunately,
is a bad word now.

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:17.190
But it's a lessening of tension.

00:41:17.190 --> 00:41:18.490
It's just what we want.

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:24.420
Now, since the last detente,
since detente has stopped,

00:41:24.420 --> 00:41:28.740
only there are even more
persecutions, and more bad

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:32.360
things, more restrictions
of civil and human rights

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:33.660
in the Soviet Union.

00:41:33.660 --> 00:41:36.690
So to be in touch with them,
to be able to talk directly

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:38.940
to them, and they're
coming here and looking

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:42.160
at the way we live, is, of
course, much more important.

00:41:42.160 --> 00:41:46.560
We should not hope that
we can change the system.

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:53.745
The last opportunity to change
a despicable political system

00:41:53.745 --> 00:41:56.150
by force was the
Second World War.

00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:58.570
And it was pretty expensive.

00:41:58.570 --> 00:42:03.410
From now on, we just can't do
it if that system is a big power

00:42:03.410 --> 00:42:05.610
and has nuclear weapons.

00:42:05.610 --> 00:42:10.430
So the only hope of changing
what happens in Russia

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:15.530
is a slow improvement,
which would probably

00:42:15.530 --> 00:42:16.730
take a long time.

00:42:16.730 --> 00:42:21.560
But it can only happen if fear,
confrontation, and distrust

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:22.910
subsides.

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:27.680
And let's see, technocrats
come to power, and who don't--

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:31.850
for whom ideological questions
are of no importance.

00:42:31.850 --> 00:42:33.860
These are the lessons
which we should

00:42:33.860 --> 00:42:38.220
draw from the sad experience
of the past 40 years,

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:42.530
when our great hopes 40
years ago for a better

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:47.630
world without conflicts
were temporarily dashed.

00:42:47.630 --> 00:42:52.680
There's still hope for
hope for the next 40 years.

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:58.000
The threat of nuclear war
has kept an uneasy peace.

00:42:58.000 --> 00:42:59.820
It has given us time.

00:42:59.820 --> 00:43:02.280
There is a meta
stable situation.

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:03.700
But it's only a meta stable.

00:43:03.700 --> 00:43:05.380
It will not last forever.

00:43:05.380 --> 00:43:07.500
So we have still time
to learn the lesson,

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:10.320
to change the present course,
which in the long run,

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:11.970
is a collision course.

00:43:11.970 --> 00:43:15.570
But this will require the
collaboration of all of you,

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:17.190
in particular the young people.

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:20.470
Because it's the young people
who will decide what happens.

00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:24.540
And if they do not participate
actively in this fight,

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:28.140
by talking, discussing,
writing to congressmen,

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:31.210
and trying to influence
colleagues, et cetera,

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:34.080
we will not get very far.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:37.170
It will require political
and military insight,

00:43:37.170 --> 00:43:40.790
and an understanding of the
psychology of the adversary,

00:43:40.790 --> 00:43:45.000
and a great deal of wisdom, and
the readiness to compromise,

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:47.310
none of which was
much in evidence

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:50.080
during the last 40 years.

00:43:50.080 --> 00:43:54.060
But progress must be based, and
all what you do must be based

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:56.640
on an absolute
pragmatic, ethical,

00:43:56.640 --> 00:44:01.620
and moral dedication to the aim
that nuclear war should not,

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:06.090
cannot, and must not occur.

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:10.770
My colleagues and I
constructed the atomic bomb.

00:44:10.770 --> 00:44:15.040
If we hadn't, other people would
have done it sooner or later.

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:19.020
What nature has made
possible cannot be avoided.

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:22.950
But the lessons we draw from
it, and what we do with it,

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:25.950
that depends on
us, not on nature.

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:27.280
Thank you.

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:30.745
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:02.125 --> 00:45:03.000
LOW: Thank you, Viki.

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:05.590
The next speaker is
Professor Philip Morrison.

00:45:05.590 --> 00:45:07.682
Phillip--

00:45:07.682 --> 00:45:10.586
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:19.790 --> 00:45:20.760
MORRISON: Thank you.

00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:24.660
I'm afraid that among the
pleasures of such discussion

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:28.340
we miss the chance for
sharp disagreement, which

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:30.740
always bring some learning.

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:33.140
We'll have to forego that,
because we are somehow

00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:35.450
too similar in point of view.

00:45:35.450 --> 00:45:38.030
I shall try not to repeat
any of the wise remarks,

00:45:38.030 --> 00:45:40.970
but to fit carefully in
between them with footnotes.

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:44.210
We'll see how that scheme works.

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:46.700
I too could begin
with a memo that

00:45:46.700 --> 00:45:48.440
came across my desk
just yesterday,

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:50.060
literally, in the mail.

00:45:50.060 --> 00:45:53.390
It was something which I
myself wrote 40 years ago.

00:45:53.390 --> 00:45:56.000
It was the quasi
official and rather

00:45:56.000 --> 00:45:59.360
over formal,
careful presentation

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:05.190
of my experiences at the Trinity
site on July 16, morning,

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:09.379
1945, which was stuck in
the files of the Los Alamos

00:46:09.379 --> 00:46:10.504
laboratory all these years.

00:46:10.504 --> 00:46:12.650
And some author has dug them
out, along with many others.

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:14.000
And he thought
I'd be interested.

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:16.050
And I was, of course,
interested to see it again.

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:18.592
I remember the main point, which
is the first paragraph which

00:46:18.592 --> 00:46:19.110
I begin.

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:21.940
It's the same memory
that Viki has.

00:46:21.940 --> 00:46:23.710
[INAUDIBLE] at base camp.

00:46:23.710 --> 00:46:28.130
At 18,000 yards, 10 miles, must
have had the same experience.

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:29.115
The site was awesome.

00:46:29.115 --> 00:46:30.365
The sound was extraordinarily.

00:46:30.365 --> 00:46:31.773
We were prepared for that.

00:46:31.773 --> 00:46:33.800
What I was somehow
not prepared for,

00:46:33.800 --> 00:46:35.270
by the calculations
and discussions

00:46:35.270 --> 00:46:39.080
we had had, was the sense
of warmth on the face,

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:41.930
as hot as summer moon,
just for a fraction

00:46:41.930 --> 00:46:44.510
of a second or a second.

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:49.770
And coming in the cold desert
morning, well before dawn,

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:52.230
this is an extraordinary
experience, indelible.

00:46:52.230 --> 00:46:54.310
Because, of course, that
kind of sensory input,

00:46:54.310 --> 00:46:56.720
with such an live
channel of input,

00:46:56.720 --> 00:46:59.400
is something that you
remember beyond the vision,

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:04.037
beyond the sound, beyond
the rational and awesome

00:47:04.037 --> 00:47:05.370
consequences that we could draw.

00:47:09.564 --> 00:47:12.850
Two of my old colleagues
at Los Alamos, one of whom

00:47:12.850 --> 00:47:15.970
I think is in this room,
have been much impressed

00:47:15.970 --> 00:47:17.470
by this same memory.

00:47:17.470 --> 00:47:20.770
And have repeatedly written and
spoken, at least tentatively,

00:47:20.770 --> 00:47:23.890
concerning the possible
wisdom of having

00:47:23.890 --> 00:47:28.060
an occasional ceremonial
above-ground test

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:30.990
of a nuclear device,
somewhere in the desert

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:36.380
or the broad Pacific, to which
we invited the heads of state

00:47:36.380 --> 00:47:40.090
or the representatives, so
they too might be at base camp,

00:47:40.090 --> 00:47:42.700
10 miles away, and feel
the heat on the face.

00:47:42.700 --> 00:47:44.980
This may be a very
ingenuous view of how

00:47:44.980 --> 00:47:46.930
statesmen minds are changed.

00:47:46.930 --> 00:47:48.610
But it is not without it's--

00:47:48.610 --> 00:47:50.400
not without its persuasiveness.

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:53.300
And I hope to hear more
about that as time goes on.

00:47:53.300 --> 00:47:56.160
Now I'd like to put the
whole thing into a little bit

00:47:56.160 --> 00:48:00.840
more history, as usual
by case studies, which

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:03.280
is the way I deal with history.

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:06.000
And I would like to
tell you something

00:48:06.000 --> 00:48:10.860
which I fell into by
the persuasiveness

00:48:10.860 --> 00:48:14.070
of another colleague, Professor
Zacharias, who is here.

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:16.650
Some years ago, maybe
a decade ago or more,

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:18.960
I was caused by his
persuasion to go to Oklahoma

00:48:18.960 --> 00:48:21.909
City, a place I rarely visit.

00:48:21.909 --> 00:48:23.761
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:24.690 --> 00:48:27.390
But in Oklahoma City,
I met a remarkable man.

00:48:27.390 --> 00:48:28.815
It was his birthday.

00:48:28.815 --> 00:48:30.190
He was a leader
of the community.

00:48:30.190 --> 00:48:33.190
I shan't describe
all his virtues.

00:48:33.190 --> 00:48:36.610
But he was a man who was then
celebrating his 100th birthday.

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:39.460
One doesn't get a chance
to meet articulate people

00:48:39.460 --> 00:48:42.610
with that kind of extensive
historical experience.

00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:44.412
And we sat in each
other at lunch.

00:48:44.412 --> 00:48:46.870
And since I was a physicist,
and he was interested in that,

00:48:46.870 --> 00:48:49.120
he told me the following
remarkable story.

00:48:49.120 --> 00:48:52.030
Which begins, in my view,
it was the beginning

00:48:52.030 --> 00:48:53.650
of the 20th century in physics.

00:48:53.650 --> 00:48:55.570
Physicists never get
things quite straight.

00:48:55.570 --> 00:49:00.290
So for us the 20th
century begins in 1896.

00:49:00.290 --> 00:49:06.320
Now, in January 1896, this
man was a young student

00:49:06.320 --> 00:49:12.130
in the freshman physics
course at Colorado College.

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:14.380
And he was professor
came in that morning,

00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:21.887
having seen the paper, and said,
the Rockaway News is coming,

00:49:21.887 --> 00:49:22.470
the newspaper.

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:23.880
Today.

00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:26.010
I'm going to cancel the
lecture I was planning,

00:49:26.010 --> 00:49:29.410
and the work that you're
supposed to do, I urge you--

00:49:29.410 --> 00:49:31.550
I invite you to work
with me this morning.

00:49:31.550 --> 00:49:33.590
And together, if
all of us work hard,

00:49:33.590 --> 00:49:36.650
we'll be able to do
something quite wonderful.

00:49:36.650 --> 00:49:40.370
Because a German professor,
far off in [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:40.370 --> 00:49:43.640
Professor Rankin
has today published

00:49:43.640 --> 00:49:47.510
how to make ras that will
allow him to take photographs

00:49:47.510 --> 00:49:50.310
of the bones in the living
hand, right through the flesh.

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:52.970
You can see the skeletal shape.

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:55.260
And his equipment
is straightforward.

00:49:55.260 --> 00:49:58.100
He describes it-- we have
that same equipment here

00:49:58.100 --> 00:49:59.570
in our shop.

00:49:59.570 --> 00:50:02.118
If we work hard,
I think by the end

00:50:02.118 --> 00:50:04.160
of the morning we'll have
accomplished this fact.

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:06.452
Perhaps, we'll be the first
in the entire United States

00:50:06.452 --> 00:50:07.670
to do so.

00:50:07.670 --> 00:50:09.950
So indeed they fell
to with a will.

00:50:09.950 --> 00:50:12.950
And my 100-year-old
acquaintance remembered

00:50:12.950 --> 00:50:15.260
going to the last
moment, to the chapel,

00:50:15.260 --> 00:50:17.060
to fetch a big
thick Bible, so they

00:50:17.060 --> 00:50:19.310
could take a photograph of
a key through a book, which

00:50:19.310 --> 00:50:22.450
is the other great
demonstration of the X-rays.

00:50:22.450 --> 00:50:24.405
And indeed, it worked
just as they said.

00:50:24.405 --> 00:50:26.030
And they were very
happy and contented.

00:50:26.030 --> 00:50:27.480
The morning was well spent.

00:50:27.480 --> 00:50:29.840
It took them in Colorado,
thousands of miles

00:50:29.840 --> 00:50:33.980
from the centers for research,
it took them a mere few hours

00:50:33.980 --> 00:50:38.870
to duplicate this
remarkable discovery.

00:50:38.870 --> 00:50:40.940
They were not the first.

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:45.130
And not the first for a
simple geographical reason.

00:50:45.130 --> 00:50:48.300
In Colorado, not because of
the indolence of the people,

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:50.160
but because of the
roundness of the Earth.

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:52.530
The sun rises two
hours later that it

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:56.460
rises in New York, Boston,
Philadelphia, and so on.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:50:59.376
Because it's on Mountain time.

00:50:59.376 --> 00:51:00.875
And therefore,
when they were just

00:51:00.875 --> 00:51:02.750
assembling, when hearing
the professor's news

00:51:02.750 --> 00:51:07.570
to the newspaper, the New
York, Boston, Philadelphia, New

00:51:07.570 --> 00:51:12.576
England, their [INAUDIBLE] had
already been at work two hours.

00:51:12.576 --> 00:51:14.610
And indeed, all across
the United States,

00:51:14.610 --> 00:51:16.910
perhaps in half
a dozen colleges,

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:19.380
in a rather provincial country,
from the point of physics

00:51:19.380 --> 00:51:25.480
research in 1896, the
X-rays had been duplicated.

00:51:25.480 --> 00:51:28.030
There's no question that
this discovery this was

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:33.940
in the medium, and it simply
required crystallization

00:51:33.940 --> 00:51:34.930
to make it appear.

00:51:34.930 --> 00:51:39.080
The nucleus was all
you had to drop in.

00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:42.455
Now, exactly the
same situation--

00:51:42.455 --> 00:51:44.330
I only realized in the
last couple of months,

00:51:44.330 --> 00:51:47.615
preparing lectures
for our course--

00:51:47.615 --> 00:51:49.740
exactly the same situation
applied to the discovery

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:51.680
of nuclear fission.

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:54.620
When fission was
described correctly

00:51:54.620 --> 00:51:57.560
by Meitner and
[INAUDIBLE] in terms

00:51:57.560 --> 00:52:02.900
that they worked out in
Christmas vacation in 1938,

00:52:02.900 --> 00:52:06.230
and was announced to the
world, across the world,

00:52:06.230 --> 00:52:11.480
from Japan, to the Soviet Union,
every place where there was

00:52:11.480 --> 00:52:15.050
a halfway respectable department
of physics with nuclear physics

00:52:15.050 --> 00:52:18.810
interests, was able to
duplicate the effect right away.

00:52:18.810 --> 00:52:21.620
And in a couple of day,
showed the fission pulses

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:24.410
on an oscilloscope to
anybody who would ask.

00:52:24.410 --> 00:52:30.060
Pulses 50 times more intense
than the pulses from alpha

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:32.060
particle emission, which
was quite common to see

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:35.070
in the same display.

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:38.550
So you see, this is the
example of two cases--

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:40.920
I know of no others
really like that--

00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:45.840
two cases where fundamental
change occurred, in a sense,

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:47.710
long overdue.

00:52:47.710 --> 00:52:49.150
The community was ready for it.

00:52:49.150 --> 00:52:50.970
The instruments had
been used widely

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:52.830
and developed for the purpose.

00:52:52.830 --> 00:52:54.630
The [INAUDIBLE] was
available everywhere.

00:52:54.630 --> 00:52:56.755
The photographic plate
was available everywhere.

00:52:56.755 --> 00:52:57.630
There was no problem.

00:52:57.630 --> 00:52:59.910
Just the right combination
had not been found.

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:02.530
And once that
combination was given,

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:04.500
the box was unlocked
all over the world.

00:53:04.500 --> 00:53:06.400
And the same thing
happened with fission.

00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:09.690
Many people failed to discover
fission in the three years

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:10.830
before it was discovered.

00:53:10.830 --> 00:53:13.080
By this or that
small failure, it

00:53:13.080 --> 00:53:16.890
was just chance led them to put
an extra piece of scotch tape

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:17.850
across the sample.

00:53:17.850 --> 00:53:19.410
That's an actual case.

00:53:19.410 --> 00:53:21.430
Not to be able to turn
the knob far enough

00:53:21.430 --> 00:53:23.970
and the design spectrograph
to catch the energy

00:53:23.970 --> 00:53:25.320
of the next X-ray line.

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:27.180
A number of other
events like that,

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:29.730
which prevented the
premature, so to speak,

00:53:29.730 --> 00:53:31.790
discovery of fission.

00:53:31.790 --> 00:53:34.250
So that in a way-- and this
is again what Viki said--

00:53:34.250 --> 00:53:37.820
the matter has the property of
the release, the large scale

00:53:37.820 --> 00:53:39.260
release of nuclear energy.

00:53:39.260 --> 00:53:46.210
And it came to us as a
extraordinary discovery.

00:53:46.210 --> 00:53:48.315
But it came, of course,
in a different context.

00:53:48.315 --> 00:53:53.430
It was not 1896, but early 1939.

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:57.450
World War began in late 1939.

00:53:57.450 --> 00:53:59.610
And was sealed,
most people feel,

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:03.150
by the Munich events
of the fall of 1938.

00:54:03.150 --> 00:54:06.810
This we felt all over, and
in California, where I was.

00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:09.480
Within a month, after we
first heard of fission, I,

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:10.850
myself, remember--

00:54:10.850 --> 00:54:12.690
and I was no expert,
and didn't understand,

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:15.120
except for the most elementary
way, the nature of neutron

00:54:15.120 --> 00:54:16.140
diffusion.

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:20.502
Drew on the board in [INAUDIBLE]
219, which we graduate students

00:54:20.502 --> 00:54:22.710
shared in the [INAUDIBLE]
room of Robert Oppenheimer,

00:54:22.710 --> 00:54:28.270
a drawing of a nuclear weapon,
with the key words-- deuterium,

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:30.430
and chain, and all those
things that you might now

00:54:30.430 --> 00:54:31.190
associate with it.

00:54:31.190 --> 00:54:32.243
It was very naive.

00:54:32.243 --> 00:54:33.160
Would not have worked.

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:34.610
But was not the result
of serious effort.

00:54:34.610 --> 00:54:35.943
It was just what was in the air.

00:54:35.943 --> 00:54:38.770
It was the climate of opinion.

00:54:38.770 --> 00:54:43.070
And so it seems very difficult
to avoid the conclusion

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:46.590
that we were in for, one way or
another, to face this problem.

00:54:46.590 --> 00:54:48.320
What did happen,
as a consequence

00:54:48.320 --> 00:54:51.740
of this strange chance,
was the close coincidence

00:54:51.740 --> 00:54:55.850
between the opening of this
possibility and the outbreak

00:54:55.850 --> 00:54:59.540
of the war, stimulated
by the most virulent,

00:54:59.540 --> 00:55:03.860
self-declared, aggressive
regime in the history of Europe

00:55:03.860 --> 00:55:05.490
for centuries.

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:08.210
This was a piece,
perhaps, of bad luck.

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:12.890
Total war was implied by
the headlines of the events.

00:55:12.890 --> 00:55:16.550
And here was the device,
embryonic device,

00:55:16.550 --> 00:55:20.610
suited intrinsically to
that particular activity.

00:55:20.610 --> 00:55:24.270
And that, perhaps, is
the historical tragedy,

00:55:24.270 --> 00:55:26.410
which we could not
have controlled,

00:55:26.410 --> 00:55:31.150
but which we certainly
have to recognize.

00:55:31.150 --> 00:55:35.300
Now, the institutions that
are responsible for this

00:55:35.300 --> 00:55:38.880
are the most dominant
institutions in our lives.

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:43.200
They have been so
for 300 years, about.

00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:45.750
That's not as old as
modern science, which

00:55:45.750 --> 00:55:48.210
goes back now about 400 years.

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:50.070
But these three
or four centuries

00:55:50.070 --> 00:55:51.840
have seen the
simultaneous growth

00:55:51.840 --> 00:55:53.610
of the two most
important institutions

00:55:53.610 --> 00:55:54.510
of contemporary life.

00:55:54.510 --> 00:55:56.250
I think it's plain
what they are.

00:55:56.250 --> 00:56:00.360
They are nations, national
states, on the one hand,

00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:04.080
and science and its conscious
application technology,

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:06.210
both ways, on the other hand.

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:10.480
These two institutions represent
the modern world in many ways.

00:56:10.480 --> 00:56:14.520
And if you look at the pages
of the books, of history,

00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:17.000
you'll see them on
every page, increasingly

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:19.130
strongly as the
centuries have come by.

00:56:23.870 --> 00:56:26.420
They have very different ideals.

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:29.130
They have very
different origins.

00:56:29.130 --> 00:56:32.480
One speaks for
diversity, universality.

00:56:32.480 --> 00:56:37.540
The other speaks for
adherence, coherence, pride

00:56:37.540 --> 00:56:40.140
in limited community.

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:42.810
True, [INAUDIBLE]
specialization has

00:56:42.810 --> 00:56:44.850
lost some of its
universality, specialization,

00:56:44.850 --> 00:56:47.000
both in place and in nature.

00:56:47.000 --> 00:56:49.340
But, of course, the national
state has, in fact, perhaps

00:56:49.340 --> 00:56:50.690
has become somewhat larger.

00:56:50.690 --> 00:56:53.690
But still, is primarily
much in the form

00:56:53.690 --> 00:56:55.820
that the revolutionaries
of France,

00:56:55.820 --> 00:56:57.420
or the United States,
for that matter,

00:56:57.420 --> 00:57:00.290
would have recognized as
the formation of a nation.

00:57:00.290 --> 00:57:02.480
And nation building
and nation forming

00:57:02.480 --> 00:57:04.250
has never been more
vigorous than it

00:57:04.250 --> 00:57:06.320
has been since the
energy released first

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:08.510
after World War I, when
the great empires of Europe

00:57:08.510 --> 00:57:12.290
crashed, and then World War
II, when the great empires that

00:57:12.290 --> 00:57:14.810
held colonial sway
across the countries

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:16.250
also crashed in their turn--

00:57:16.250 --> 00:57:21.170
Holland, the British
Commonwealth, and so on.

00:57:21.170 --> 00:57:22.790
Now, in the days
of the enlightened,

00:57:22.790 --> 00:57:27.730
it's hard to believe how
these two institutions fitted

00:57:27.730 --> 00:57:28.700
with each other.

00:57:28.700 --> 00:57:31.180
And it's very difficult
to satisfy yourself

00:57:31.180 --> 00:57:34.348
that it's the same way,
from the present day,

00:57:34.348 --> 00:57:35.140
that it's that way.

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:36.175
But it was that way.

00:57:36.175 --> 00:57:37.300
And there are many reasons.

00:57:37.300 --> 00:57:38.500
I shan't elaborate that much.

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:39.833
You can see them for yourselves.

00:57:39.833 --> 00:57:42.200
But I just wanted
to mention two.

00:57:42.200 --> 00:57:45.110
When Captain Cook set sail
on his wonderful expedition

00:57:45.110 --> 00:57:47.840
to explore the-- to look
for a Southern continent,

00:57:47.840 --> 00:57:50.780
measure the transit
of Venus, and explore

00:57:50.780 --> 00:57:58.670
the Pacific for His
Majesty, our country,

00:57:58.670 --> 00:58:01.600
which was at war with Great
Britain, a Revolutionary

00:58:01.600 --> 00:58:07.720
War in full energy, through its
brilliant scientific ambassador

00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:12.430
to Paris, Mr. Franklin, wrote
a letter to all our commanders

00:58:12.430 --> 00:58:16.860
at sea, saying, by no means
attack Captain Cook's vessel.

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:18.130
Give him free passage.

00:58:18.130 --> 00:58:20.890
He sails not as a
British ship, not as

00:58:20.890 --> 00:58:24.970
a ship of the British Navy, but
as a representative of science.

00:58:24.970 --> 00:58:27.760
What he does will only
enhance the virtues

00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:30.640
to all mankind, including
the United States of America,

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:32.680
and gave him safe conduct.

00:58:32.680 --> 00:58:36.350
It's hard to believe
that would happen today.

00:58:36.350 --> 00:58:40.760
Even later, even later when
Colonel William Lambton was

00:58:40.760 --> 00:58:42.980
engaged by the East
India Company, not even

00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:45.680
the British state, but
an agent of the state,

00:58:45.680 --> 00:58:49.730
the East India Company, to begin
the great triangulation, which

00:58:49.730 --> 00:58:52.370
has led to the
modern Indian survey,

00:58:52.370 --> 00:58:56.410
beautifully mapped from
Himalayas to the Cape,

00:58:56.410 --> 00:59:00.310
his great fundamental
theodolite that

00:59:00.310 --> 00:59:02.500
was to be used to establish
the principle geodetic

00:59:02.500 --> 00:59:08.960
coordinates, was sent by sea
and captured by a French ship--

00:59:08.960 --> 00:59:11.240
a disaster.

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:12.000
No.

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:13.790
Three or four years
later, no great delay

00:59:13.790 --> 00:59:18.960
in the circumstances of
1808 or 1810, when it was,

00:59:18.960 --> 00:59:22.470
it appeared, by courtesy
of the French Academy, who

00:59:22.470 --> 00:59:25.050
said they had no interest
in depriving Colonel Lambon

00:59:25.050 --> 00:59:26.300
of his wonderful instrument.

00:59:26.300 --> 00:59:27.180
And there was.

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:29.820
Please use it and
publish the maps.

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.910
Well, it was a time you
may say was ingenuous.

00:59:31.910 --> 00:59:34.860
Of course, it was a time when
science did not have the power,

00:59:34.860 --> 00:59:36.637
did not generate the power yet.

00:59:36.637 --> 00:59:38.220
The power was, of
course, in the hands

00:59:38.220 --> 00:59:40.350
of armorers, always had been.

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:42.870
But it was technology,
and not yet much

00:59:42.870 --> 00:59:46.830
influenced by the more
rational abstract qualities

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:50.890
that we see in
post-Galilean science.

00:59:50.890 --> 00:59:53.620
Nowadays, of course,
the figure of the earth

00:59:53.620 --> 00:59:55.573
is a top military secret.

00:59:55.573 --> 00:59:57.490
And if you know where
there are concentrations

00:59:57.490 --> 00:59:59.770
of mass and changes in
the geode that haven't yet

00:59:59.770 --> 01:00:01.890
been worked out from the
satellite perturbations,

01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:04.060
you better not tell
anybody, because it's

01:00:04.060 --> 01:00:07.160
very important for
directing the missiles.

01:00:07.160 --> 01:00:10.000
And against it is
this exponential rise

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:12.100
of a powerful means
of understanding,

01:00:12.100 --> 01:00:15.100
and thus controlling,
features the environment.

01:00:15.100 --> 01:00:18.992
These two great
changes interlock.

01:00:18.992 --> 01:00:20.950
And something new entered
with nuclear weapons,

01:00:20.950 --> 01:00:23.080
which I maintain are special.

01:00:23.080 --> 01:00:24.710
It's a dangerous
position to maintain,

01:00:24.710 --> 01:00:25.918
but I think it is a real one.

01:00:25.918 --> 01:00:29.150
It is verifiable in some
sense quantitatively.

01:00:29.150 --> 01:00:33.030
They are not ordinary.

01:00:33.030 --> 01:00:36.270
People have always wondered
at the expressions of energy.

01:00:36.270 --> 01:00:38.260
And of course, in terms
of sheer total energy,

01:00:38.260 --> 01:00:42.210
we still can't match the
energy released in nature.

01:00:42.210 --> 01:00:47.080
The philosophers of Hellenic
and Hellenistic times

01:00:47.080 --> 01:00:50.140
recognized energy and
gravitation, falling bodies,

01:00:50.140 --> 01:00:52.120
in the winds, in
the water currents,

01:00:52.120 --> 01:00:53.362
the waves of the ocean.

01:00:53.362 --> 01:00:55.570
Of course, they don't have
the power density which we

01:00:55.570 --> 01:00:59.690
command in nuclear explosions.

01:00:59.690 --> 01:01:01.990
They recognized a
second kind of energy,

01:01:01.990 --> 01:01:05.583
animate energy, the
energy characteristic

01:01:05.583 --> 01:01:07.000
of human beings,
their own motion,

01:01:07.000 --> 01:01:08.560
and the motion of
all the animals.

01:01:08.560 --> 01:01:10.420
In fact that sign
of motion, we still

01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:13.650
speak of something
animated when it is moving.

01:01:13.650 --> 01:01:16.380
And the notion that mechanical
objects would also move

01:01:16.380 --> 01:01:19.980
came as a strange
and marvelous thing.

01:01:19.980 --> 01:01:22.630
2,000 years ago, and even
five 300 or 400 years ago,

01:01:22.630 --> 01:01:24.750
it was still quite
remarkable, that motion

01:01:24.750 --> 01:01:28.560
could be transferred from
living things or mere forces

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:31.140
of nature, where it
was intrinsic to move

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:35.910
like wind and waves, to
little clockwork objects.

01:01:35.910 --> 01:01:38.580
We've lost that
sense entirely today.

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:40.780
So I think I said earlier,
in this very room,

01:01:40.780 --> 01:01:42.510
there's a parallel
today, which has

01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:45.340
to do with intelligence
in mechanical objects.

01:01:45.340 --> 01:01:48.217
And there too, people are
puzzled and associated.

01:01:48.217 --> 01:01:50.550
I think it's very likely the
same consequences to occur.

01:01:50.550 --> 01:01:53.520
Namely, it will be
transferred to mechanisms.

01:01:53.520 --> 01:01:55.320
And we won't understand
those mechanisms.

01:01:55.320 --> 01:01:57.862
Those mechanism will not enable
us to understand intelligence

01:01:57.862 --> 01:02:00.180
in ourselves, any more than
the motion of windup mice

01:02:00.180 --> 01:02:02.310
enable us to understand
mice very well.

01:02:02.310 --> 01:02:03.900
It's a very small
part of the problem.

01:02:06.678 --> 01:02:10.150
But the power in
nuclear explosives,

01:02:10.150 --> 01:02:13.090
as everyone's pointed
out, is extraordinary.

01:02:13.090 --> 01:02:14.718
But this is the
fundamental point.

01:02:14.718 --> 01:02:16.510
The fundamental point
is not only the power

01:02:16.510 --> 01:02:17.170
that can be summoned there.

01:02:17.170 --> 01:02:18.712
Of course, if you
pile up enough TNT,

01:02:18.712 --> 01:02:20.680
you can pile up the
same amount of energy.

01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:21.908
But you can't do that.

01:02:21.908 --> 01:02:23.200
You can't do that economically.

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:25.960
It is the
extraordinary cheapness

01:02:25.960 --> 01:02:28.810
of nuclear energy
in explosive form

01:02:28.810 --> 01:02:31.965
that is the characteristic
danger of the situation.

01:02:34.880 --> 01:02:37.520
It must be 20 years ago, I
was riding in an airplane one

01:02:37.520 --> 01:02:39.920
night, late flight.

01:02:39.920 --> 01:02:43.770
And I had bought a copy of
a familiar magazine, The New

01:02:43.770 --> 01:02:45.630
Yorker, looking at the cartoons.

01:02:45.630 --> 01:02:47.670
I was startled to
find a cartoon which

01:02:47.670 --> 01:02:52.450
was based on the following
conceit of a clever cartoonist.

01:02:52.450 --> 01:02:58.350
Was a time Soviet-Chinese
differences, in the early '60s.

01:02:58.350 --> 01:03:01.430
And the cartoon showed
a gentleman, [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:01.430 --> 01:03:04.910
of the Chinese, a Mandarin--
one of the Mandarin [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:04.910 --> 01:03:07.820
learned Mandarin [INAUDIBLE],,
impassive in his robes,

01:03:07.820 --> 01:03:09.230
and waving the appropriate fan.

01:03:09.230 --> 01:03:11.450
It's all made very chinoiserie.

01:03:11.450 --> 01:03:14.720
And then a bunch of toiling
people in their blue uniforms--

01:03:14.720 --> 01:03:16.310
you could see that--

01:03:16.310 --> 01:03:19.030
making a great firecracker.

01:03:19.030 --> 01:03:24.150
And the caption said,
they test, we test.

01:03:24.150 --> 01:03:26.990
And that was the idea
of the cartoonist.

01:03:26.990 --> 01:03:28.560
Now, the firecracker
was gigantic.

01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:33.190
It was about as big as a house,
35 or 40 feet in diameter,

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:36.000
and it was drawn perhaps
150, 200 feet long.

01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:38.790
And toiling men were carrying
a great thing to light it.

01:03:38.790 --> 01:03:41.990
You can see the exaggeration
of the cartoonist.

01:03:41.990 --> 01:03:44.120
But you recognize
that if I were to try

01:03:44.120 --> 01:03:47.900
to make a megaton of gunpowder,
which must be what they used

01:03:47.900 --> 01:03:50.690
of firecrackers, the
firecracker to represent that

01:03:50.690 --> 01:03:52.930
would have to be a 30
foot diameter tube.

01:03:52.930 --> 01:03:56.240
It would not just be 200 feet
long, or 2,000 feet long,

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:58.820
or two miles long,
or 20 miles long.

01:03:58.820 --> 01:04:00.750
It would actually go
right across the horizon.

01:04:00.750 --> 01:04:02.810
Add a few megatons, and
disappear into space,

01:04:02.810 --> 01:04:06.318
like the Great Wall,
narrowing down to [INAUDIBLE]..

01:04:06.318 --> 01:04:07.283
It's hyperbolic.

01:04:07.283 --> 01:04:08.700
The cartoonist is
a better artist.

01:04:08.700 --> 01:04:11.500
He knew if he drew that, people
would think it was impossible,

01:04:11.500 --> 01:04:13.810
but it's simple sense.

01:04:13.810 --> 01:04:15.830
And that's the
trouble, and very hard

01:04:15.830 --> 01:04:19.418
to imagine that trouble if you
can't calculate it and face it.

01:04:19.418 --> 01:04:21.710
And even when you've seen
it, it's not easy to imagine.

01:04:21.710 --> 01:04:24.230
But that the situation.

01:04:24.230 --> 01:04:26.330
As everyone knows, the
statistic-- a freight

01:04:26.330 --> 01:04:29.050
train loaded with boxcars
of TNT, if it rattles by,

01:04:29.050 --> 01:04:32.270
it will carry a megaton by
at full speed part your grade

01:04:32.270 --> 01:04:33.425
crossing in eight hours.

01:04:36.670 --> 01:04:37.390
That's a megaton.

01:04:40.380 --> 01:04:43.750
The disposition of the
forces now gives each side

01:04:43.750 --> 01:04:49.810
a yield of between 5 and 10
Geiger tons, 5,000 or 10,000

01:04:49.810 --> 01:04:51.988
megatons each side.

01:04:51.988 --> 01:04:52.780
This is inordinate.

01:04:52.780 --> 01:04:54.822
It doesn't compare to any
of the other structures

01:04:54.822 --> 01:04:55.752
in our society.

01:04:55.752 --> 01:04:58.210
And that's why I think it is
fair to say that it represents

01:04:58.210 --> 01:05:05.520
a novel danger, not to be
compared to the terrible

01:05:05.520 --> 01:05:09.480
history of war between nations,
which indeed is bad enough,

01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:14.220
and in human terms cannot
be dismissed or diminished.

01:05:14.220 --> 01:05:17.933
Who is to think otherwise, when
to read all these grave things.

01:05:17.933 --> 01:05:19.350
Even though at the
same time, they

01:05:19.350 --> 01:05:22.170
brought out courage,
and sacrifice, and unity

01:05:22.170 --> 01:05:27.600
in the usual way that
nationalism has built for us.

01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:30.150
Who could not hope
for an end to war?

01:05:30.150 --> 01:05:35.170
Who would not dream of
[INAUDIBLE] of all time?

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:36.475
Ever since we've had states--

01:05:36.475 --> 01:05:38.350
it's not intrinsic
probably to human beings--

01:05:38.350 --> 01:05:40.850
but to the
organization in states.

01:05:40.850 --> 01:05:42.850
But I think we have--

01:05:42.850 --> 01:05:46.570
at the moment, I offer
a more modest goal.

01:05:46.570 --> 01:05:48.396
It is the abolition of nuclear.

01:05:48.396 --> 01:05:51.800
Which is not the same thing
as the abolition of war,

01:05:51.800 --> 01:05:53.780
or the settlement of all
national differences,

01:05:53.780 --> 01:05:55.430
or indeed, the common
agreement, which

01:05:55.430 --> 01:05:57.200
we must try to seek and widen.

01:05:57.200 --> 01:06:01.280
But I recognize that's an
old and longstanding problem.

01:06:01.280 --> 01:06:04.280
And short of accomplishment
of just distribution

01:06:04.280 --> 01:06:06.680
of the economic
goods of the world,

01:06:06.680 --> 01:06:09.080
it seems to be very hard,
at least, to guarantee it.

01:06:09.080 --> 01:06:12.770
And short of solving
the long histories which

01:06:12.770 --> 01:06:20.010
imply the words of revenge,
and retribution, and so on,

01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:23.280
the classic arguments
of the past,

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:26.250
I don't see how we can hope
to bring those to clear end.

01:06:26.250 --> 01:06:29.370
But we can, I think,
separate out the idea

01:06:29.370 --> 01:06:32.670
that nuclear war will not
serve any of these interests.

01:06:32.670 --> 01:06:37.080
Nuclear war will not advance
the concerns of nations.

01:06:37.080 --> 01:06:42.570
And it's only use, if it has
any use at all in the arsenals,

01:06:42.570 --> 01:06:45.070
is as a deterrent against
nuclear war itself.

01:06:45.070 --> 01:06:51.080
That's to say, as a hedge
against the fear that

01:06:51.080 --> 01:06:56.150
surreptitious weapons could
be found, could be made,

01:06:56.150 --> 01:06:58.970
which would represent a
violation of agreement,

01:06:58.970 --> 01:07:02.930
and thus, bring danger.

01:07:02.930 --> 01:07:06.640
And if you hold a small
threshold, a small amount,

01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:11.730
which is enough to cover any
plausible concealment, then

01:07:11.730 --> 01:07:15.240
indeed, that too
brings no advantage.

01:07:15.240 --> 01:07:18.260
And could be expected to be
irrational and not much worried

01:07:18.260 --> 01:07:19.140
about.

01:07:19.140 --> 01:07:21.180
So that I think is the
end we have to look at,

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:22.890
the proximate end.

01:07:22.890 --> 01:07:24.660
I don't deny, and I
would hope that we

01:07:24.660 --> 01:07:26.200
can do much better than that.

01:07:26.200 --> 01:07:27.160
But that is far enough.

01:07:27.160 --> 01:07:28.827
It's much farther
than where we are now.

01:07:28.827 --> 01:07:30.327
Because where we
are now, of course,

01:07:30.327 --> 01:07:31.650
is a very clear position.

01:07:31.650 --> 01:07:33.290
The statesmen have
by their action,

01:07:33.290 --> 01:07:34.830
rather by their
inaction, in spite

01:07:34.830 --> 01:07:37.350
of many provocations,
and many differences,

01:07:37.350 --> 01:07:39.390
have refrained from
direct confrontation

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:42.510
between adversaries heavily
armed with nuclear weapons.

01:07:42.510 --> 01:07:46.500
And that's because there is a
strong inhibition to their use,

01:07:46.500 --> 01:07:49.230
a strong sense of
survival, and this

01:07:49.230 --> 01:07:51.113
we can count all to the good.

01:07:51.113 --> 01:07:52.530
But what they have
intended to say

01:07:52.530 --> 01:07:55.608
is there will only be
no disaster on my watch.

01:07:55.608 --> 01:07:57.900
Therefore, we'll build more
missiles, but not use them.

01:07:57.900 --> 01:08:01.290
And the next group can decide
how to solve the matter.

01:08:01.290 --> 01:08:03.330
That's been going
on for 40 years.

01:08:03.330 --> 01:08:06.090
The justifications for
this system and that system

01:08:06.090 --> 01:08:10.530
are so thin and so
self-contradictory,

01:08:10.530 --> 01:08:14.130
as you read them over the
years, that we're just

01:08:14.130 --> 01:08:16.200
doing the study
now in our course,

01:08:16.200 --> 01:08:18.270
and it comes ever
clearer that there

01:08:18.270 --> 01:08:22.479
is no way on the level
of individual decisions

01:08:22.479 --> 01:08:23.979
to say that they
are right or wrong.

01:08:23.979 --> 01:08:25.021
They're never very right.

01:08:25.021 --> 01:08:27.840
They're always
tendentiously argued.

01:08:27.840 --> 01:08:30.510
The essential
point appears to be

01:08:30.510 --> 01:08:34.229
that states will spend
what they can spend,

01:08:34.229 --> 01:08:37.140
5% or 10% of the gross
national product,

01:08:37.140 --> 01:08:38.790
on military affairs,
whatever that

01:08:38.790 --> 01:08:40.620
buys when they're
[INAUDIBLE] a state

01:08:40.620 --> 01:08:42.180
of adversarial relationship.

01:08:42.180 --> 01:08:43.229
Whatever that buys.

01:08:43.229 --> 01:08:45.870
They don't ask what it buys,
they ask what can they spend.

01:08:45.870 --> 01:08:47.640
It's the level of
effort that counts.

01:08:47.640 --> 01:08:49.410
We have two million
men in armed services.

01:08:49.410 --> 01:08:51.702
We've always had two million
men in the armed services,

01:08:51.702 --> 01:08:52.439
since 1950.

01:08:52.439 --> 01:08:53.840
And that's what it is.

01:08:53.840 --> 01:08:55.200
And they can be armed.

01:08:55.200 --> 01:08:56.729
However powerfully
they're armed,

01:08:56.729 --> 01:08:59.760
it doesn't really matter, as
long as the expenditures are

01:08:59.760 --> 01:09:00.990
about the same.

01:09:00.990 --> 01:09:03.750
When we had 100s
or 1,000 bombers

01:09:03.750 --> 01:09:08.576
flying three tons of bombs
each, or whether we have 1,000--

01:09:08.576 --> 01:09:11.700
300 in this case, bombers,
and another 1,000 missiles,

01:09:11.700 --> 01:09:14.798
with the equivalent of
a million tons each,

01:09:14.798 --> 01:09:15.840
it really doesn't matter.

01:09:15.840 --> 01:09:17.720
The cost is much the same.

01:09:17.720 --> 01:09:20.040
And it's that which is
looked at by the states

01:09:20.040 --> 01:09:21.592
and by their
[INAUDIBLE] And there's

01:09:21.592 --> 01:09:23.550
no other explanation for
the order of magnitude

01:09:23.550 --> 01:09:24.569
of what we've built up.

01:09:24.569 --> 01:09:26.152
It's not measured
in terms of targets.

01:09:26.152 --> 01:09:27.689
It's measured on
the input you need.

01:09:27.689 --> 01:09:29.130
The input you need
is so and so much.

01:09:29.130 --> 01:09:29.880
That's reasonable.

01:09:29.880 --> 01:09:31.290
We always spent
that kind of sum.

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:32.540
Let's spend it again.

01:09:32.540 --> 01:09:34.540
And it doesn't matter the
technicians are making

01:09:34.540 --> 01:09:35.970
it easier to have the damage.

01:09:35.970 --> 01:09:37.140
That's the tragedy.

01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:39.660
It's not looked at in terms of
output, but in terms of input

01:09:39.660 --> 01:09:40.160
alone.

01:09:42.520 --> 01:09:46.410
Other technologies
will come along,

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:49.710
which will again engage this
conflict between technology

01:09:49.710 --> 01:09:50.850
and the nation state.

01:09:50.850 --> 01:09:57.710
I don't know which ones, but I'm
confident that the one we see

01:09:57.710 --> 01:10:00.860
is sufficiently grave so
that we have to take action.

01:10:03.830 --> 01:10:06.080
A new point has arisen.

01:10:06.080 --> 01:10:08.960
It's not new, again, it's
a repetition of events,

01:10:08.960 --> 01:10:13.250
as Viki said, of
1969 to 1972 or '73,

01:10:13.250 --> 01:10:17.000
when the idea of a defense
against the delivery

01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:21.360
of nuclear weapons was
in the air, literally.

01:10:21.360 --> 01:10:23.680
And now, it's gone from
the air to outer space

01:10:23.680 --> 01:10:27.992
for two of the three layers,
but still much the same thing.

01:10:27.992 --> 01:10:29.450
And the trouble
with it, of course,

01:10:29.450 --> 01:10:32.690
is not the detail of
the systems, not at all.

01:10:32.690 --> 01:10:35.470
I don't want to discuss
X-ray lasers, or ground

01:10:35.470 --> 01:10:37.833
based chemical lasers, or
whatever you have in mind.

01:10:37.833 --> 01:10:40.000
Those are all interesting
and important discussions.

01:10:40.000 --> 01:10:41.390
We should have them here.

01:10:41.390 --> 01:10:43.090
But they don't-- in
this institution--

01:10:43.090 --> 01:10:44.650
they don't represent
the real issue.

01:10:44.650 --> 01:10:46.120
Because the real
issue is not that.

01:10:46.120 --> 01:10:50.240
The real issue is that a defense
system, genuine defense--

01:10:50.240 --> 01:10:52.880
that is active defense,
not deterrence,

01:10:52.880 --> 01:10:55.750
nuclear weapons style--

01:10:55.750 --> 01:11:01.120
has always been and remains
based upon marginal superiority

01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:02.920
of an installed system.

01:11:02.920 --> 01:11:05.770
My sword will
pierce your shield.

01:11:05.770 --> 01:11:10.150
Not every time, you're
not shield-less,

01:11:10.150 --> 01:11:12.850
but the decision has
always been in the margin.

01:11:12.850 --> 01:11:14.780
Some little difference
has made a difference.

01:11:14.780 --> 01:11:21.000
The shells penetrated the deck
of one side, but not the other.

01:11:21.000 --> 01:11:23.270
And the remaining ships that
floated were of one kind.

01:11:23.270 --> 01:11:24.440
They won the battle.

01:11:24.440 --> 01:11:26.270
It's always in this
marginal distinction

01:11:26.270 --> 01:11:30.430
that every defense,
offense war has been won.

01:11:30.430 --> 01:11:32.930
And that will be true for
strategic defense initiatives,

01:11:32.930 --> 01:11:34.310
in space, anywhere.

01:11:34.310 --> 01:11:36.290
The general picture
is not the medium

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:37.680
that makes the difference.

01:11:37.680 --> 01:11:40.502
It's the energy
and technical means

01:11:40.502 --> 01:11:41.960
placed at the
disposal of those who

01:11:41.960 --> 01:11:44.510
will counter any given system.

01:11:44.510 --> 01:11:47.180
So we can expect, since
we've always seen it before,

01:11:47.180 --> 01:11:49.460
a seesaw jockeying, which
now this will be hit,

01:11:49.460 --> 01:11:50.640
now that will be hit.

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:52.610
And on top of that,
always the proposition

01:11:52.610 --> 01:11:55.220
that nuclear weapons are
still inordinately powerful,

01:11:55.220 --> 01:11:57.800
and a small leakage of
them through any defense

01:11:57.800 --> 01:12:00.170
is devastating.

01:12:00.170 --> 01:12:03.583
So you really, as everyone
said, raised another race.

01:12:03.583 --> 01:12:05.500
But you've not solved
the fundamental problem.

01:12:05.500 --> 01:12:07.900
The difference, again, is
this extraordinary order

01:12:07.900 --> 01:12:10.210
of magnitude that
nuclear energy introduces

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:12.268
that none of the other
devices introduce at all.

01:12:12.268 --> 01:12:13.810
Because they always
have their match.

01:12:13.810 --> 01:12:16.640
But not nuclear energy,
there's no match.

01:12:16.640 --> 01:12:20.200
I'm afraid that just as
we've seen with the B52,

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:23.410
which has only been used in
combat against a non-nuclear

01:12:23.410 --> 01:12:25.560
power--

01:12:25.560 --> 01:12:29.880
three of them-- Cambodia,
Laos, and Vietnam,

01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:31.430
and the MX missile,
which was built

01:12:31.430 --> 01:12:34.040
for quite a different
purpose than its present use,

01:12:34.040 --> 01:12:35.600
to close the window
of vulnerability,

01:12:35.600 --> 01:12:38.540
and skitter around the
Southwestern desert,

01:12:38.540 --> 01:12:43.600
to avoid its targetability,
imitating the submarines,

01:12:43.600 --> 01:12:44.240
abandoned.

01:12:44.240 --> 01:12:47.600
No such justification
is present any longer.

01:12:47.600 --> 01:12:50.350
It's just meant as a better
missile with a hard target kill

01:12:50.350 --> 01:12:53.230
capability, to add
to our present force.

01:12:53.230 --> 01:12:57.070
And so I believe, in fact, that
the SDI system, when it finally

01:12:57.070 --> 01:12:59.250
is built, and has
its terminal defense

01:12:59.250 --> 01:13:00.880
and a few things
in orbit and so on,

01:13:00.880 --> 01:13:02.710
will not at all be
regarded as adequate

01:13:02.710 --> 01:13:05.890
against the powerful
countermeasures and increased

01:13:05.890 --> 01:13:09.840
missile powers of the powerful
superpower against us,

01:13:09.840 --> 01:13:12.730
but will be used on the
side to threaten and perhaps

01:13:12.730 --> 01:13:15.190
even to strike at
somebody somewhere else

01:13:15.190 --> 01:13:17.920
in the world, who hasn't got any
kind of anti-missile capability

01:13:17.920 --> 01:13:19.530
or satellites or anything else.

01:13:19.530 --> 01:13:22.630
Because against such a power,
it is indeed a powerful weapon,

01:13:22.630 --> 01:13:25.330
not perhaps a winning weapon,
though very powerful activity,

01:13:25.330 --> 01:13:27.790
just as the 52s were very--

01:13:27.790 --> 01:13:29.880
dropped a lot of bombs
through the monsoon

01:13:29.880 --> 01:13:31.030
are Laos and Cambodia.

01:13:31.030 --> 01:13:32.980
Whether they won anything,
it's hard to say,

01:13:32.980 --> 01:13:35.383
but they certainly blew
up a lot of territory.

01:13:35.383 --> 01:13:37.300
And I'm afraid that's
what we're going to see.

01:13:37.300 --> 01:13:39.250
Missile systems have
this curious life,

01:13:39.250 --> 01:13:40.810
or systems have
this curious life.

01:13:40.810 --> 01:13:43.060
Once built, their
utility depends on what

01:13:43.060 --> 01:13:45.515
you choose to do with them.

01:13:45.515 --> 01:13:46.890
And when they're
badly conceived,

01:13:46.890 --> 01:13:48.890
then they're not useful
for the primary purpose.

01:13:48.890 --> 01:13:51.057
And they are so expensive,
you couldn't build them

01:13:51.057 --> 01:13:52.140
for the secondary purpose.

01:13:52.140 --> 01:13:53.510
But you've got them.

01:13:53.510 --> 01:13:56.212
So there they are.

01:13:56.212 --> 01:13:57.420
I think that's the situation.

01:13:57.420 --> 01:14:01.380
And I believe the solution has
got to be measures of restraint

01:14:01.380 --> 01:14:05.290
as an initial break
to the situation.

01:14:05.290 --> 01:14:12.130
Restraint, not just negotiated,
long negotiated mutuality.

01:14:12.130 --> 01:14:16.510
Rapidly produced measures
of unilateral restraint,

01:14:16.510 --> 01:14:18.000
using that word.

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:19.905
And I say this, I
wanted to say it,

01:14:19.905 --> 01:14:24.760
but I got a letter today,
which I'll just comment on,

01:14:24.760 --> 01:14:28.563
a form letter, of course, from
a very able Congresswoman,

01:14:28.563 --> 01:14:30.730
Patricia Schroeder, of the
Armed Services Committee,

01:14:30.730 --> 01:14:33.310
a Congresswoman from Colorado.

01:14:33.310 --> 01:14:34.990
And she proposes the
following scheme.

01:14:34.990 --> 01:14:35.920
Now, I have no way--

01:14:35.920 --> 01:14:37.390
I haven't talked
to enough people

01:14:37.390 --> 01:14:39.145
to say this is a good
idea or a bad idea.

01:14:39.145 --> 01:14:40.270
It appeals to me very much.

01:14:40.270 --> 01:14:43.510
I'm sure it is the flavor of
what eventually will work.

01:14:43.510 --> 01:14:46.870
And I put it forward to
you only and that means.

01:14:46.870 --> 01:14:48.010
It depends upon timing.

01:14:48.010 --> 01:14:49.290
It depends upon unity.

01:14:49.290 --> 01:14:51.260
It depends on political
circumstances.

01:14:51.260 --> 01:14:53.650
Maybe none of these
are propitious.

01:14:53.650 --> 01:14:55.950
But the idea is a sound one.

01:14:55.950 --> 01:14:56.920
I'll just mention it.

01:14:56.920 --> 01:15:01.550
She says, let it be the sense
of Congress that on August 6--

01:15:01.550 --> 01:15:04.550
before August 6,
1985, our president

01:15:04.550 --> 01:15:08.760
shall announce a three
month withholding

01:15:08.760 --> 01:15:12.470
of all nuclear
tests on condition

01:15:12.470 --> 01:15:15.450
only that the Russians
do the same thing.

01:15:15.450 --> 01:15:17.420
And if they do, if
he does not certify

01:15:17.420 --> 01:15:19.610
that they have made a
test, then the Congress

01:15:19.610 --> 01:15:21.110
will not allow any
money to be spent

01:15:21.110 --> 01:15:23.360
on this for such
purposes, indefinitely,

01:15:23.360 --> 01:15:25.530
looking at it every
six months or a year.

01:15:25.530 --> 01:15:27.500
Meanwhile, negotiations
begin on a more

01:15:27.500 --> 01:15:30.380
formidable and comprehensive
test [INAUDIBLE]..

01:15:30.380 --> 01:15:31.820
I think this is interesting.

01:15:31.820 --> 01:15:34.345
Very mild restraint,
because you're not

01:15:34.345 --> 01:15:36.710
going to lose very much
under any circumstance.

01:15:36.710 --> 01:15:38.060
It's verifiable.

01:15:38.060 --> 01:15:41.210
And it has the quality
of an initiative, which

01:15:41.210 --> 01:15:44.900
is what we need,
something to start with,

01:15:44.900 --> 01:15:47.810
not waiting to see whether the
bargaining chips on the table

01:15:47.810 --> 01:15:49.090
can be counted up well.

01:15:49.090 --> 01:15:52.460
So unless we find such
initiatives of restraint,

01:15:52.460 --> 01:15:55.040
unless we find such
minor sacrifices,

01:15:55.040 --> 01:15:58.550
minor risks to take in the face
of this growing, steady, never

01:15:58.550 --> 01:16:01.220
decisive, but always
terrible risk, which

01:16:01.220 --> 01:16:05.460
is the single minded
arms race for greater

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:08.322
and greater destruction, then
I think we'll find no way out.

01:16:08.322 --> 01:16:09.780
And I believe one
side or the other

01:16:09.780 --> 01:16:12.262
must try this, once,
twice, many times,

01:16:12.262 --> 01:16:13.470
until it becomes [INAUDIBLE].

01:16:13.470 --> 01:16:17.070
Always careful not to
compromise the peace

01:16:17.070 --> 01:16:19.200
in any substantial
way, by the small,

01:16:19.200 --> 01:16:24.430
but clearly indicated
direction of its proposals.

01:16:24.430 --> 01:16:27.700
So I close then, very
much in the same way

01:16:27.700 --> 01:16:31.260
that I would have
closed the talk in 1945,

01:16:31.260 --> 01:16:35.430
showing that one doesn't
learn very much in this world.

01:16:35.430 --> 01:16:40.590
But when we came back
from New Mexico and Japan,

01:16:40.590 --> 01:16:46.260
the scientists, ingenuous,
but energetic, as usual,

01:16:46.260 --> 01:16:50.420
made up slogans for what should
be done in the crisis of 1945.

01:16:50.420 --> 01:16:51.920
And I think the
slogans are not bad.

01:16:51.920 --> 01:16:55.140
And I'd like to repeat
them again in this time,

01:16:55.140 --> 01:16:57.580
with a little
explanation and end.

01:16:57.580 --> 01:17:00.900
First of all, we said there
is no secret to this thing.

01:17:00.900 --> 01:17:03.420
That's a foregone
conclusion now.

01:17:03.420 --> 01:17:06.050
Nobody imagines there is.

01:17:06.050 --> 01:17:07.055
It's all over the world.

01:17:07.055 --> 01:17:09.680
But of course, in those days, it
was a big political statement,

01:17:09.680 --> 01:17:12.410
because the notion was
we would hold the secret

01:17:12.410 --> 01:17:16.790
and we alone, with the UK, would
be the sole nuclear powers.

01:17:16.790 --> 01:17:18.110
We said that will not work.

01:17:18.110 --> 01:17:20.290
The secret will fade.

01:17:20.290 --> 01:17:22.040
The secret is held in
the uranium nucleus,

01:17:22.040 --> 01:17:24.830
and that is everywhere.

01:17:24.830 --> 01:17:27.650
Second, we said there
will be no defense,

01:17:27.650 --> 01:17:29.960
not because we knew about
X-ray lasers, we didn't.

01:17:29.960 --> 01:17:32.043
Because we knew the simple
argument I made before,

01:17:32.043 --> 01:17:34.850
that defense is in the
margin, but nuclear weapons

01:17:34.850 --> 01:17:37.030
are beyond that.

01:17:37.030 --> 01:17:38.855
And finally, we said
that it seems there's

01:17:38.855 --> 01:17:40.440
only one way to proceed.

01:17:40.440 --> 01:17:43.850
And that is, somehow by
eventual international agreement

01:17:43.850 --> 01:17:47.730
to do away, as far as we
can, with nuclear war.

01:17:47.730 --> 01:17:48.769
Thank you.

01:17:48.769 --> 01:17:53.260
[APPLAUSE]

01:18:26.250 --> 01:18:32.320
LOW: Phil and Viki, thank
you for incredible talks.

01:18:32.320 --> 01:18:36.600
Mr. Bajpai would you honor
us with some remarks?

01:18:36.600 --> 01:18:39.026
Mr. Bajpai is the
Indian ambassador.

01:18:39.026 --> 01:18:42.810
[APPLAUSE]

01:18:46.121 --> 01:18:49.220
BAJPAI: Provost
Low and friends, I

01:18:49.220 --> 01:18:52.646
find myself in
double embarrassment

01:18:52.646 --> 01:18:56.240
that almost everything I
would have liked to say

01:18:56.240 --> 01:18:59.510
has been so much better
said, not only by these two

01:18:59.510 --> 01:19:03.350
greats because we have just
heard, but more briefly,

01:19:03.350 --> 01:19:07.850
but very much to the point
also, in the collection that you

01:19:07.850 --> 01:19:12.260
have in the pamphlet
that is with you.

01:19:12.260 --> 01:19:15.510
My other embarrassment
is, quite candidly,

01:19:15.510 --> 01:19:20.960
that I look like being suspect
of joining dangerous company,

01:19:20.960 --> 01:19:23.930
because we all
feel the same way.

01:19:23.930 --> 01:19:26.210
And the way they
feel is not very

01:19:26.210 --> 01:19:28.880
popular in certain quarters.

01:19:28.880 --> 01:19:30.740
And I rather fear
that we may all

01:19:30.740 --> 01:19:35.041
be accused of conspiring
together to corrupt the young.

01:19:35.041 --> 01:19:37.200
[LAUGHTER]

01:19:37.200 --> 01:19:41.690
And to subvert the international
system, as the powers that are

01:19:41.690 --> 01:19:42.720
prefer to see it.

01:19:42.720 --> 01:19:47.770
And subvert the existing
or accepted prevailing

01:19:47.770 --> 01:19:52.120
wisdom, in Washington at least.

01:19:52.120 --> 01:19:57.550
I must confess that the
presence of a layman like myself

01:19:57.550 --> 01:20:02.770
in this very remarkable
gathering of scientists

01:20:02.770 --> 01:20:07.110
must raise two questions, at
least in my mind, if not yours,

01:20:07.110 --> 01:20:12.260
is why I was included, and
the other is why I came.

01:20:16.370 --> 01:20:19.110
The first I would--

01:20:19.110 --> 01:20:22.380
I'm sure is not because
the father of the atom bomb

01:20:22.380 --> 01:20:24.390
quoted one of our
scriptures when the atom

01:20:24.390 --> 01:20:27.670
bomb was first exploded.

01:20:27.670 --> 01:20:30.570
And I hope it is not
just because India

01:20:30.570 --> 01:20:35.040
is one of the countries in
the world that has developed

01:20:35.040 --> 01:20:38.740
the know how of nuclear energy.

01:20:38.740 --> 01:20:44.370
I wish I could think it was
an acknowledgment of India's

01:20:44.370 --> 01:20:50.100
commitment to this cause
of finding solutions

01:20:50.100 --> 01:20:52.950
to this terrible
fear that we have all

01:20:52.950 --> 01:20:57.460
lived under in these 40 years.

01:20:57.460 --> 01:21:05.120
It is a remarkable coincidence,
in a way, that this nuclear age

01:21:05.120 --> 01:21:06.470
is almost--

01:21:06.470 --> 01:21:10.570
at the same time, began at
the same time, as India's life

01:21:10.570 --> 01:21:13.600
as a modern state.

01:21:13.600 --> 01:21:19.660
It was a day of dread
and glory for all of us.

01:21:19.660 --> 01:21:22.350
And the tragedy of those of us
who come from the developing

01:21:22.350 --> 01:21:27.320
world is that the glory we
had hoped to achieve from it

01:21:27.320 --> 01:21:30.540
has yet to manifest itself.

01:21:30.540 --> 01:21:32.590
And the dread is
all too pervasive.

01:21:37.630 --> 01:21:40.850
I, of course, have come
because we continue

01:21:40.850 --> 01:21:44.780
to attach this very
high importance,

01:21:44.780 --> 01:21:48.470
and have from the very beginning
of our life as a member

01:21:48.470 --> 01:21:51.260
of the international
community, tried

01:21:51.260 --> 01:21:56.450
to work for a solution,
which after a very few years,

01:21:56.450 --> 01:22:00.120
we came to believe [INAUDIBLE]
complete and general

01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:05.220
disarmament, starting
with the total abolition

01:22:05.220 --> 01:22:08.350
of all nuclear weapons.

01:22:08.350 --> 01:22:10.470
We have been pressing
for this since we first

01:22:10.470 --> 01:22:15.430
launched the proposal in
the United Nations in 1954.

01:22:15.430 --> 01:22:18.500
And followed it up with other
proposals about banning tests,

01:22:18.500 --> 01:22:24.950
about stopping vertical, as well
as horizontal, proliferation.

01:22:24.950 --> 01:22:30.650
And indeed, about outlying
nuclear weapons altogether.

01:22:30.650 --> 01:22:34.430
We haven't gotten
very far, as you know.

01:22:34.430 --> 01:22:38.370
And there's yet
another anniversary

01:22:38.370 --> 01:22:41.610
that we will be
observing this year.

01:22:41.610 --> 01:22:45.930
It is the 10th anniversary of
the non-proliferation treaty.

01:22:45.930 --> 01:22:48.600
And many have said,
all right, if you

01:22:48.600 --> 01:22:51.090
can't manage the totality
of the danger, at least

01:22:51.090 --> 01:22:57.380
let us limit it by going
through the self-denial process

01:22:57.380 --> 01:23:02.110
of those countries that have
not yet developed the bomb.

01:23:02.110 --> 01:23:05.790
We have not signed the
Nonproliferation Treaty,

01:23:05.790 --> 01:23:10.430
although we have advocated
its purposes and intentions.

01:23:10.430 --> 01:23:14.210
And I think that is
because of a factor that

01:23:14.210 --> 01:23:20.270
has been mentioned in passing
or indirectly earlier today.

01:23:22.790 --> 01:23:26.450
The nation state has probably
been responsible for more wars

01:23:26.450 --> 01:23:31.030
than almost any other
single factor in history.

01:23:31.030 --> 01:23:34.480
And yet, we, who
became nation states,

01:23:34.480 --> 01:23:36.100
only a few years
ago, are much too

01:23:36.100 --> 01:23:41.140
jealous and proud of that
fact to want a different order

01:23:41.140 --> 01:23:43.970
of international affairs.

01:23:43.970 --> 01:23:46.442
But what we have always
wanted is a different order

01:23:46.442 --> 01:23:47.525
of international behavior.

01:23:50.310 --> 01:23:52.790
I don't know how many of you
saw the film this afternoon,

01:23:52.790 --> 01:23:55.880
but one of those who
was interviewed in it

01:23:55.880 --> 01:24:01.380
had an interesting
recollection that the capacity

01:24:01.380 --> 01:24:07.730
to produce this weapon really
came to fruition after Germany

01:24:07.730 --> 01:24:10.390
had already surrendered.

01:24:10.390 --> 01:24:13.840
And I believe you all had a
debate as to whether to go

01:24:13.840 --> 01:24:17.240
ahead or not.

01:24:17.240 --> 01:24:20.385
And as I think--

01:24:20.385 --> 01:24:24.420
who was it that was
recollecting this?

01:24:24.420 --> 01:24:28.890
Professor Wilson-- he
mentioned that talking

01:24:28.890 --> 01:24:34.080
to Dr. Oppenheimer, they
argued back and forth.

01:24:34.080 --> 01:24:36.840
And Oppenheimer
said, no, this is

01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:39.660
the age of the United Nations.

01:24:39.660 --> 01:24:41.760
And the United Nations
is going to usher

01:24:41.760 --> 01:24:44.790
in a new world order
which must know

01:24:44.790 --> 01:24:48.030
what this dreadful power is.

01:24:48.030 --> 01:24:49.950
I take it, therefore,
that the scientists

01:24:49.950 --> 01:24:54.300
of that time, as I see know from
the scientists of today, did

01:24:54.300 --> 01:24:58.890
have in mind a very
different way of nations

01:24:58.890 --> 01:25:01.560
dealing with each other.

01:25:01.560 --> 01:25:04.030
And if this implies some
surrender of sovereignty,

01:25:04.030 --> 01:25:07.680
I am sure it would be acceptable
to nearly every country I can

01:25:07.680 --> 01:25:10.530
think of, provided every
other country would do it

01:25:10.530 --> 01:25:12.180
at the same time.

01:25:12.180 --> 01:25:16.320
And that, of course, is what
nobody ever is prepared to do.

01:25:16.320 --> 01:25:21.270
We have, therefore,
this continuing problem

01:25:21.270 --> 01:25:25.830
of a few states wanting
to retain their monopoly

01:25:25.830 --> 01:25:31.750
and telling the rest of us
that we should be good boys.

01:25:31.750 --> 01:25:34.040
You'll pointed out,
Professor Weisskopf,

01:25:34.040 --> 01:25:38.200
that the nuclear
powers have somehow

01:25:38.200 --> 01:25:40.680
managed to keep the
peace between themselves

01:25:40.680 --> 01:25:45.810
for 40 years, which is an
exceptionally long time.

01:25:45.810 --> 01:25:50.070
I am reminded that an
almost equal span of time

01:25:50.070 --> 01:25:54.770
prevailed between the Congress
of Vienna and the Crimea War.

01:25:54.770 --> 01:25:58.680
And yet, during that period,
it was the smaller nations

01:25:58.680 --> 01:26:00.990
who were denied their rights.

01:26:00.990 --> 01:26:02.610
And the concert of
Europe, which ran

01:26:02.610 --> 01:26:05.280
what was then called
the civilized world,

01:26:05.280 --> 01:26:09.390
did manage to keep peace
between the powers that

01:26:09.390 --> 01:26:12.700
formed that concert, but by
sowing the seeds for what

01:26:12.700 --> 01:26:16.780
proved to be the
greatest wars of history.

01:26:16.780 --> 01:26:20.830
I do not think we would like to
see that experience repeated.

01:26:20.830 --> 01:26:24.230
So this so-called
balance of terror,

01:26:24.230 --> 01:26:28.430
which is trying to keep the
world at peace through fear,

01:26:28.430 --> 01:26:31.690
has now reached this
ridiculous proportion,

01:26:31.690 --> 01:26:34.370
where you keep piling up.

01:26:34.370 --> 01:26:37.910
And the scales maybe
perhaps able to maintain

01:26:37.910 --> 01:26:39.940
that equilibrium.

01:26:39.940 --> 01:26:43.040
But if you keep piling
up, the scales may break.

01:26:43.040 --> 01:26:45.660
And then where will we be?

01:26:45.660 --> 01:26:48.300
So those of us who had
looked for the peaceful uses

01:26:48.300 --> 01:26:51.570
of nuclear development, and
which we hoped would enable us,

01:26:51.570 --> 01:26:54.620
particularly coming from
a country like India,

01:26:54.620 --> 01:26:58.170
with its vast potential,
but also its vast problems,

01:26:58.170 --> 01:27:01.740
would help us give to our people
the type of life that you had

01:27:01.740 --> 01:27:05.520
built for yourselves, cannot
help wonder whether it's worth

01:27:05.520 --> 01:27:10.200
the effort, and whether we must
not now think of other ways

01:27:10.200 --> 01:27:12.780
of achieving that.

01:27:12.780 --> 01:27:16.620
As far as this is concerned,
we have persisted, as I say,

01:27:16.620 --> 01:27:22.410
in pressing for total
abolition of nuclear weapons.

01:27:22.410 --> 01:27:27.720
And in the process, we have
been told that we are naive.

01:27:27.720 --> 01:27:29.990
The fact of life is that
a few countries have it

01:27:29.990 --> 01:27:31.642
and they will manage.

01:27:31.642 --> 01:27:35.940
And the rest of us, as
I said, better behave.

01:27:35.940 --> 01:27:41.760
And others have gone so far
as to say that we are devious,

01:27:41.760 --> 01:27:44.200
that while we maintain this
posture on the one hand,

01:27:44.200 --> 01:27:46.990
we do not sign the
NPT on the other.

01:27:46.990 --> 01:27:49.260
And that we are merely
allowing ourselves the option

01:27:49.260 --> 01:27:54.320
to use the nuclear energy
for military purposes when

01:27:54.320 --> 01:27:55.376
we want to.

01:27:55.376 --> 01:28:00.160
I would on that, just mention
one word, that our record is

01:28:00.160 --> 01:28:01.780
there for everyone to see.

01:28:01.780 --> 01:28:03.610
We have demonstrated
a capability,

01:28:03.610 --> 01:28:05.680
but not developed it.

01:28:05.680 --> 01:28:08.160
And we do keep urging others
to follow our example.

01:28:10.890 --> 01:28:16.130
The epithets that are used
used to be applied to a man

01:28:16.130 --> 01:28:22.790
called Gandhi, whom the British
thought on the one hand naive

01:28:22.790 --> 01:28:25.481
and on the other devious.

01:28:25.481 --> 01:28:29.230
I think most, if not
all of us here today

01:28:29.230 --> 01:28:35.270
would agree that looking back
on his life and his achievement,

01:28:35.270 --> 01:28:40.030
he was able to do something very
new, which was to make people

01:28:40.030 --> 01:28:42.780
behave in a different way.

01:28:42.780 --> 01:28:49.510
Certainly, we in India,
somehow under his leadership,

01:28:49.510 --> 01:28:55.780
rose to a level of loyalty
of devotion of self-denial,

01:28:55.780 --> 01:28:57.610
which probably we
can never do again,

01:28:57.610 --> 01:29:01.170
until another
Gandhi comes along.

01:29:01.170 --> 01:29:04.250
But it did give us the hope
that the rest of mankind

01:29:04.250 --> 01:29:07.070
can under competent leadership,
or inspiring leadership,

01:29:07.070 --> 01:29:10.583
achieve that same
type of change.

01:29:10.583 --> 01:29:12.000
And what we are
really thinking of

01:29:12.000 --> 01:29:14.340
is to change men's
minds so that nations

01:29:14.340 --> 01:29:17.930
can change their ways of
dealing with each other.

01:29:17.930 --> 01:29:21.890
And we still believe
that that is possible.

01:29:21.890 --> 01:29:25.610
We do not have a
Gandhi to offer you,

01:29:25.610 --> 01:29:29.570
but you have so many people
who can speak that language,

01:29:29.570 --> 01:29:32.830
perhaps we don't need him.

01:29:32.830 --> 01:29:36.480
But I would like to say
that the type of ideas

01:29:36.480 --> 01:29:38.760
that I've heard today
are so much what

01:29:38.760 --> 01:29:40.610
we have tried to advocate.

01:29:40.610 --> 01:29:44.910
It is very heartening to find
circles in the United States

01:29:44.910 --> 01:29:47.720
that I can feel at
home in this matter.

01:29:47.720 --> 01:29:50.710
But above all, I
would say I think

01:29:50.710 --> 01:29:54.780
it was another great man of
science, Sir William Oslo, who

01:29:54.780 --> 01:30:00.640
used to say, that in science,
it does not matter who

01:30:00.640 --> 01:30:03.780
first conceives of
an idea, what matters

01:30:03.780 --> 01:30:06.590
is who will convince the world.

01:30:06.590 --> 01:30:09.350
I'm afraid we in India
have not found it

01:30:09.350 --> 01:30:11.900
possible to convince the world.

01:30:11.900 --> 01:30:15.080
I hope all of your scientists,
and above all, all of you

01:30:15.080 --> 01:30:18.330
young people here
today, will succeed.

01:30:18.330 --> 01:30:20.510
Thank you very much.

01:30:20.510 --> 01:30:23.510
[APPLAUSE]

01:30:50.510 --> 01:30:54.280
LOW: Thank you for
your eloquent remarks.

01:30:54.280 --> 01:30:58.270
I think we have time for
some comments and questions,

01:30:58.270 --> 01:31:00.400
not too many.

01:31:00.400 --> 01:31:02.990
Sir--

01:31:02.990 --> 01:31:06.824
AUDIENCE: I was very happy
to hear he mention of Gandhi.

01:31:06.824 --> 01:31:10.110
I'm very impressed
with the possibilities

01:31:10.110 --> 01:31:12.460
of non-violence myself.

01:31:12.460 --> 01:31:18.580
Most of the comments from the
speakers about what can be done

01:31:18.580 --> 01:31:22.990
were dealt with on a
Congressional, or executive,

01:31:22.990 --> 01:31:26.420
or policy levels, and I'm just
wondering if the distinguished

01:31:26.420 --> 01:31:31.521
speakers here could
comment on what value

01:31:31.521 --> 01:31:34.876
they see in
non-violent resistance

01:31:34.876 --> 01:31:51.470
as a way of furthering ourselves
for the goal of [INAUDIBLE]

01:31:51.470 --> 01:31:56.760
WEISSKOPF: Well, yes, I would
see a lot of possibilities.

01:31:56.760 --> 01:31:59.250
You know, at the
end of my talk, I

01:31:59.250 --> 01:32:02.340
said that it really
depends on all of us,

01:32:02.340 --> 01:32:04.590
in particular on
the young people,

01:32:04.590 --> 01:32:07.950
on the younger
generation, to establish

01:32:07.950 --> 01:32:15.840
this change of attitude
towards our opponents, that

01:32:15.840 --> 01:32:22.120
would make and would avoid
the terrible possibilities.

01:32:22.120 --> 01:32:27.450
Now, this change of attitude,
how do you get at it?

01:32:27.450 --> 01:32:30.210
By demonstrations,
by speaking out.

01:32:30.210 --> 01:32:35.030
And this is a kind of
non-violent resistance.

01:32:40.330 --> 01:32:43.780
I'm sure some people will
not like it, what I say,

01:32:43.780 --> 01:32:50.320
but the kind of demonstrations
to prevent certain weapons,

01:32:50.320 --> 01:32:53.680
well, they usually do not at
the end really prevent them,

01:32:53.680 --> 01:32:57.760
but I think they have a very
important purpose of showing

01:32:57.760 --> 01:33:01.930
the ideas that are in the
population, the resistance,

01:33:01.930 --> 01:33:04.300
for example, in Europe
against the erection

01:33:04.300 --> 01:33:10.490
of those intermediate arrange
things and similar thing.

01:33:10.490 --> 01:33:13.120
In other words, although
it is in principle

01:33:13.120 --> 01:33:16.600
against the law,
these demonstrations,

01:33:16.600 --> 01:33:20.400
as long as they
are not violent, I

01:33:20.400 --> 01:33:23.610
think they are one
of those expressions

01:33:23.610 --> 01:33:25.650
that I think would help.

01:33:25.650 --> 01:33:27.520
You cannot do it only that way.

01:33:27.520 --> 01:33:30.540
You also have to convince,
you have to argue,

01:33:30.540 --> 01:33:32.720
you have to get the
majority on your side.

01:33:32.720 --> 01:33:35.730
But certainly nonviolent
demonstrations,

01:33:35.730 --> 01:33:38.232
if I understand
that's what you mean--

01:33:38.232 --> 01:33:39.607
AUDIENCE: Civil
disobedience-- do

01:33:39.607 --> 01:33:41.470
you see a role for
civil disobedience?

01:33:41.470 --> 01:33:43.280
WEISSKOPF: Yes,
in certain cases,

01:33:43.280 --> 01:33:46.150
it may just be a
demonstration of the strength

01:33:46.150 --> 01:33:47.536
of your convictions.

01:33:54.373 --> 01:33:55.790
AUDIENCE: Something
that I thought

01:33:55.790 --> 01:33:58.310
might be talked about this
evening, that I think perhaps

01:33:58.310 --> 01:34:03.110
we should touch on a little
bit is the consideration

01:34:03.110 --> 01:34:05.510
that weapons require builders.

01:34:05.510 --> 01:34:08.660
And as we all know, MIT
is one of the places

01:34:08.660 --> 01:34:13.190
where much of the building
of these weapons gets done.

01:34:13.190 --> 01:34:16.465
Ideally, perhaps, decisions
about whether to build weapons

01:34:16.465 --> 01:34:17.840
as political
decisions should not

01:34:17.840 --> 01:34:21.200
have to concern
scientists and engineers.

01:34:21.200 --> 01:34:24.800
But given the fact that the
government and our politicians

01:34:24.800 --> 01:34:29.390
continue to be as irrational
as you two pointed out,

01:34:29.390 --> 01:34:31.620
I think we are forced
to think about it.

01:34:31.620 --> 01:34:35.240
And so I want to ask you, as
a student who may or may not

01:34:35.240 --> 01:34:37.933
go into science,
especially in a world where

01:34:37.933 --> 01:34:40.100
so much of the funding is
only available for defense

01:34:40.100 --> 01:34:43.710
work, how should we
think about this problem,

01:34:43.710 --> 01:34:46.135
and especially considering
the fact that we're at MIT?

01:34:46.135 --> 01:34:46.760
And the title--

01:34:53.100 --> 01:34:55.200
MORRISON: Of course,
it's not a new--

01:34:55.200 --> 01:34:59.520
it's a difficult question,
but hardly a new one.

01:34:59.520 --> 01:35:03.580
I'm afraid I take a very
social view of the situation.

01:35:03.580 --> 01:35:08.670
I'm not against, but hardly
enthusiastic for solutions

01:35:08.670 --> 01:35:12.760
that mean the thing goes
on just as well elsewhere.

01:35:12.760 --> 01:35:15.240
Or the thing goes
on, but without me.

01:35:15.240 --> 01:35:17.250
These are valuable
answers for individuals.

01:35:17.250 --> 01:35:19.270
And I would never deny
any person that right.

01:35:19.270 --> 01:35:22.380
It's indeed a
necessity to work out

01:35:22.380 --> 01:35:25.680
for every individual,
the comfort, the degree

01:35:25.680 --> 01:35:29.690
of opposition to the
national direction

01:35:29.690 --> 01:35:32.580
that that person
wants to stand in.

01:35:32.580 --> 01:35:36.720
But really, to move a laboratory
from one place to another,

01:35:36.720 --> 01:35:40.620
or to do something
[INAUDIBLE] is

01:35:40.620 --> 01:35:43.140
I think primarily an evasion,
unless it is used to organize

01:35:43.140 --> 01:35:46.330
opinion in a broader sense.

01:35:46.330 --> 01:35:48.750
So I'm not quite sure
that I'm speaking directly

01:35:48.750 --> 01:35:52.020
to your point, but I
think what I'm seeking is,

01:35:52.020 --> 01:35:56.850
how to deny the
power to the state,

01:35:56.850 --> 01:35:58.320
to manufacture more
or less what it

01:35:58.320 --> 01:36:02.640
wants to do, I think it's
very difficult for us,

01:36:02.640 --> 01:36:05.102
unless a large majority
of people agree on it.

01:36:05.102 --> 01:36:07.560
Then I think there's very little
chance of succeeding along

01:36:07.560 --> 01:36:08.067
those lines.

01:36:08.067 --> 01:36:09.900
And if they do agree,
the institutions still

01:36:09.900 --> 01:36:13.770
exist to use the power of
the state to withhold it,

01:36:13.770 --> 01:36:16.427
not to spend the money, not
to organize the factories,

01:36:16.427 --> 01:36:17.760
to sign the treaties, and so on.

01:36:17.760 --> 01:36:22.620
I feel this is a the direction
which the solution will lie.

01:36:22.620 --> 01:36:24.270
That doesn't exclude
that everyone must

01:36:24.270 --> 01:36:31.110
find a personal relationship.

01:36:31.110 --> 01:36:34.245
WEISSKOPF: May I add something?

01:36:34.245 --> 01:36:39.060
See MIT essentially
represents the United States.

01:36:39.060 --> 01:36:40.650
I would even say it--

01:36:40.650 --> 01:36:42.720
actually, there are
probably more people

01:36:42.720 --> 01:36:45.300
against the present--

01:36:45.300 --> 01:36:49.230
percent wise-- against
the present trend at MIT.

01:36:49.230 --> 01:36:52.890
But in general, MIT
represents, and should

01:36:52.890 --> 01:36:53.850
represent the nation.

01:36:53.850 --> 01:36:58.563
Because if we at MIT exclude
every weapon work, then--

01:36:58.563 --> 01:37:00.480
well, I'm now a little
repeating what he said.

01:37:00.480 --> 01:37:03.760
Then we would be an ivory tower
who would have no influence.

01:37:03.760 --> 01:37:06.150
So in some ways,
I would say, sure,

01:37:06.150 --> 01:37:07.860
it's your own decision
whether you will

01:37:07.860 --> 01:37:11.300
go into that work or this work.

01:37:11.300 --> 01:37:16.430
But let me assure you, if you
want to become a scientist,

01:37:16.430 --> 01:37:18.650
you do not do it in
order to earn money.

01:37:18.650 --> 01:37:21.030
Of course, you will earn
more money in defense.

01:37:21.030 --> 01:37:24.170
But the few examples
which I have given

01:37:24.170 --> 01:37:27.050
have shown that there are
other very important tasks

01:37:27.050 --> 01:37:29.090
for science.

01:37:29.090 --> 01:37:30.970
You know the examples.

01:37:30.970 --> 01:37:34.270
And I think, therefore,
we need scientists.

01:37:34.270 --> 01:37:36.730
And we need even
intelligent scientists

01:37:36.730 --> 01:37:38.568
in the weapons departments.

01:37:38.568 --> 01:37:41.110
Because they can then have an
influence there, and say, look,

01:37:41.110 --> 01:37:43.360
what you do here is nonsense.

01:37:43.360 --> 01:37:47.950
Even weapon developments
that are stabilizing and not

01:37:47.950 --> 01:37:53.060
destabilizing, like some of
these so-called smart, not

01:37:53.060 --> 01:37:54.000
nuclear weapon.

01:37:54.000 --> 01:37:56.710
So things it's not that easy.

01:37:56.710 --> 01:38:00.080
But certainly, you should
not use this as a reason

01:38:00.080 --> 01:38:02.330
not to go into
science, that science

01:38:02.330 --> 01:38:05.910
is abused for nuclear war.

01:38:05.910 --> 01:38:07.790
LOW: Also, just to keep
the record straight,

01:38:07.790 --> 01:38:11.480
one should say, MIT does
not do weapons work.

01:38:11.480 --> 01:38:15.140
At the Lincoln Lab, which is
a quite separate installation,

01:38:15.140 --> 01:38:21.130
and therefore, historic
reasons, radar work is done.

01:38:21.130 --> 01:38:25.210
But MIT, as a university here,
does not do weapons work.

01:38:25.210 --> 01:38:29.410
In any case, it does
not do any secret work.

01:38:29.410 --> 01:38:32.945
And once you leave
the domain of secrecy,

01:38:32.945 --> 01:38:37.150
it's hard to know what is
a weapon and what is not.

01:38:37.150 --> 01:38:42.320
But certainly, no weapons
are designed here.

01:38:42.320 --> 01:38:44.380
I'll add another thing.

01:38:47.560 --> 01:38:52.630
I think an individual has
to decide to be a pacifist

01:38:52.630 --> 01:38:53.910
or not to be a pacifist.

01:38:53.910 --> 01:38:58.840
That is to say, if you oppose
war, then it is logical for you

01:38:58.840 --> 01:39:00.370
to oppose weapons work.

01:39:00.370 --> 01:39:04.670
If not, then somebody
has to do that work.

01:39:04.670 --> 01:39:07.420
So I think it's not
intrinsically immoral,

01:39:07.420 --> 01:39:09.430
any more than war
itself is immoral,

01:39:09.430 --> 01:39:11.540
and our societies
do engage in it.

01:39:11.540 --> 01:39:15.280
So I think to give yourself
the luxury of saying

01:39:15.280 --> 01:39:16.990
I'm not going to
indulge in weapons work,

01:39:16.990 --> 01:39:19.120
and then no community I
have anything to do with

01:39:19.120 --> 01:39:23.618
should be involved in it, is
not, at least not consistent,

01:39:23.618 --> 01:39:24.660
unless you're a pacifist.

01:39:31.030 --> 01:39:35.810
AUDIENCE: To continue the point
that Scott Zaleski just made,

01:39:35.810 --> 01:39:40.020
I think the question that I've
heard from a number of students

01:39:40.020 --> 01:39:41.590
is whether--

01:39:41.590 --> 01:39:44.650
let me back up and say, I
think that all three responses

01:39:44.650 --> 01:39:46.565
suggest, and I
think quite rightly,

01:39:46.565 --> 01:39:50.070
there's an issue of some
personal moral responsibility

01:39:50.070 --> 01:39:53.190
and judgment as
to what one can do

01:39:53.190 --> 01:39:57.030
who wants to go into science.

01:39:57.030 --> 01:40:00.330
But I think that those of us
who are members of the faculty

01:40:00.330 --> 01:40:03.420
and administration
at MIT perhaps or are

01:40:03.420 --> 01:40:09.310
being told by the
[INAUDIBLE] that it's

01:40:09.310 --> 01:40:10.900
a little lonely out there.

01:40:10.900 --> 01:40:12.650
You're a young person.

01:40:12.650 --> 01:40:15.520
You're facing
choices in which you

01:40:15.520 --> 01:40:19.900
begin to discover that much
of what is interesting--

01:40:19.900 --> 01:40:23.190
regarded as interesting
and exciting, much of what

01:40:23.190 --> 01:40:27.030
is regarded as well-paid is
[INAUDIBLE] only by making

01:40:27.030 --> 01:40:31.810
choices [INAUDIBLE] whether it's
done at MIT, or by extension

01:40:31.810 --> 01:40:32.750
MIT graduates.

01:40:32.750 --> 01:40:37.260
And I guess the follow-on
to Scott Zaleski's question

01:40:37.260 --> 01:40:40.550
that I would like to
ask is, are there things

01:40:40.550 --> 01:40:44.600
that an institution such
as this could and should

01:40:44.600 --> 01:40:48.940
do to encourage the development
of alternative pathways

01:40:48.940 --> 01:40:52.530
of career choices, so
those in large numbers,

01:40:52.530 --> 01:40:56.280
of our students, who would if
they could make choices not

01:40:56.280 --> 01:40:57.777
to do weapons
research, but perhaps

01:40:57.777 --> 01:40:59.610
to work more actively
on the sorts of things

01:40:59.610 --> 01:41:03.800
that Viki mentioned in
his quite reasonable list.

01:41:03.800 --> 01:41:08.403
I'm trying to suggest that maybe
it's a little unfair for us

01:41:08.403 --> 01:41:11.120
to reduce the whole matter to
a question of personal choice.

01:41:11.120 --> 01:41:15.205
There are institutional
priorities [INAUDIBLE]

01:41:15.205 --> 01:41:18.642
[APPLAUSE]

01:41:25.030 --> 01:41:27.170
LOW: Steve, I agree with that.

01:41:27.170 --> 01:41:30.680
And, in fact, the Institution
is moving in those directions.

01:41:30.680 --> 01:41:32.320
There are people
in this Institution

01:41:32.320 --> 01:41:36.430
who are hard at work on exactly
the kinds of questions--

01:41:36.430 --> 01:41:40.270
kinds of movement that
Viki and Phil describe.

01:41:40.270 --> 01:41:41.233
You know them.

01:41:41.233 --> 01:41:42.400
They're here on the faculty.

01:41:42.400 --> 01:41:44.770
And we are making every
effort to support them

01:41:44.770 --> 01:41:46.540
and to expand that work.

01:41:46.540 --> 01:41:47.870
And we hope it will bear fruit.

01:41:47.870 --> 01:41:50.800
And we hope that there
will be new ideas.

01:41:50.800 --> 01:41:55.065
And that students and members of
the community will be involved.

01:41:55.065 --> 01:41:55.690
We're doing it.

01:41:58.910 --> 01:42:02.480
AUDIENCE: Excuse me, may
I get your attention?

01:42:02.480 --> 01:42:04.710
I think I have a sort of
somewhat appropriate remark

01:42:04.710 --> 01:42:05.210
to make.

01:42:05.210 --> 01:42:06.950
It's not really a
question, but it's

01:42:06.950 --> 01:42:10.850
sort of more or less a
statement or a request.

01:42:10.850 --> 01:42:12.710
This afternoon,
Professor Martin Deutsch

01:42:12.710 --> 01:42:16.890
said that in 1945 the human
race was forced, so to speak,

01:42:16.890 --> 01:42:18.800
into adulthood by the bomb.

01:42:18.800 --> 01:42:20.300
It occurred to me
at that time, that

01:42:20.300 --> 01:42:22.790
to reverse the direction
of this nuclear madness,

01:42:22.790 --> 01:42:24.980
we, as individual
adults, will have

01:42:24.980 --> 01:42:28.010
to band together and meet a
challenge at least as great

01:42:28.010 --> 01:42:31.130
as that which threatened
the nation and the world

01:42:31.130 --> 01:42:33.140
back during World War II.

01:42:33.140 --> 01:42:35.390
And perhaps in that
regard, I wanted

01:42:35.390 --> 01:42:38.960
to say that a small group of
students and staff here at MIT

01:42:38.960 --> 01:42:41.330
have been organizing
some efforts to provide

01:42:41.330 --> 01:42:43.190
additional information
to students

01:42:43.190 --> 01:42:45.740
who are preparing to make
occupational decisions as they

01:42:45.740 --> 01:42:47.383
finish up at MIT.

01:42:47.383 --> 01:42:49.550
Our preliminary focus has
been to talk with students

01:42:49.550 --> 01:42:51.920
who are considering
taking jobs with companies

01:42:51.920 --> 01:42:55.490
whose principal work is on
nuclear weapons systems.

01:42:55.490 --> 01:42:58.220
We want to continue
this work and extend it.

01:42:58.220 --> 01:43:02.040
Among the goals we
have is, for example,

01:43:02.040 --> 01:43:05.660
to provide an up to date and
comprehensive set of reference

01:43:05.660 --> 01:43:09.620
materials to be located in the
MIT Career Placement Office,

01:43:09.620 --> 01:43:11.990
so that students who use
the office in a job search

01:43:11.990 --> 01:43:15.080
have access to a concise
and accurate alternative

01:43:15.080 --> 01:43:18.020
view of the many large defense
contractors who currently

01:43:18.020 --> 01:43:20.990
recruit and provide shiny,
new, attractive brochures

01:43:20.990 --> 01:43:22.925
on themselves at the
placement office.

01:43:22.925 --> 01:43:24.350
This is just short.

01:43:24.350 --> 01:43:26.540
We need help.

01:43:26.540 --> 01:43:29.960
We need help, fresh ideas,
blood, sweat, and tears

01:43:29.960 --> 01:43:32.830
to make an impact
at the Institute.

01:43:32.830 --> 01:43:36.237
Our small efforts this semester
have been very encouraging.

01:43:36.237 --> 01:43:38.570
It turns out that there are
a lot of students who really

01:43:38.570 --> 01:43:42.350
haven't thought, and don't have
resources available to them

01:43:42.350 --> 01:43:46.490
with their many problem sets,
and with all the interviews

01:43:46.490 --> 01:43:48.710
that they have to go through
in getting out of here,

01:43:48.710 --> 01:43:52.580
they really haven't had any
resources available to them

01:43:52.580 --> 01:43:56.210
to help them make more moral
or ethical choices about jobs.

01:43:56.210 --> 01:43:59.240
So finally, this summer
will be an ideal time

01:43:59.240 --> 01:44:02.090
to begin our planning for
the fall recruiting period.

01:44:02.090 --> 01:44:04.770
If you're interested, any
of you, in getting involved,

01:44:04.770 --> 01:44:06.850
we need people who
can read and write,

01:44:06.850 --> 01:44:10.640
who like research, and have
good ideas and enthusiasm.

01:44:10.640 --> 01:44:11.930
Please get in touch with me.

01:44:11.930 --> 01:44:13.460
My name is Ken Finkelstein.

01:44:13.460 --> 01:44:16.130
You can reach me by leaving a
note at the Graduate Physics

01:44:16.130 --> 01:44:16.688
Office.

01:44:16.688 --> 01:44:18.980
That's Ken Finkelstein, and
we're the Student Committee

01:44:18.980 --> 01:44:20.400
on Career Choices.

01:44:20.400 --> 01:44:21.100
Thank you.

01:44:21.100 --> 01:44:23.920
[APPLAUSE]

01:44:28.150 --> 01:44:31.580
LOW: I think we have
come to the end.

01:44:31.580 --> 01:44:33.440
Oh, I'm sorry, did
you have a question?

01:44:33.440 --> 01:44:34.590
AUDIENCE: Oh, yeah, I
did have a question,

01:44:34.590 --> 01:44:36.777
but it's a little bit on a
slightly different issue.

01:44:36.777 --> 01:44:38.360
So if there's anybody
who still wanted

01:44:38.360 --> 01:44:40.640
to talk about the
career thing, I

01:44:40.640 --> 01:44:43.165
want to wait a couple seconds.

01:44:43.165 --> 01:44:45.040
OK, well, if there's
not, I'll just go ahead.

01:44:45.040 --> 01:44:49.730
And first of all, Mr.
Ambassador, please corrupt me.

01:44:49.730 --> 01:44:50.640
I love it.

01:44:50.640 --> 01:44:54.710
But in any case, it
was sort of a question

01:44:54.710 --> 01:44:57.280
for Professor Morrison.

01:44:57.280 --> 01:45:03.230
When you were talking in terms
of the nuclear and conventional

01:45:03.230 --> 01:45:09.540
war differences and how we
might be able to adjust to the--

01:45:09.540 --> 01:45:12.722
adjust to going back to a
conventional war situation,

01:45:12.722 --> 01:45:14.180
I'm not quite sure
I got you right.

01:45:14.180 --> 01:45:18.020
But is it your opinion
that it's in any way

01:45:18.020 --> 01:45:22.460
realistic to assume that
a nation would cut back

01:45:22.460 --> 01:45:29.300
on nuclear arms, cut back on
them and then sort of just

01:45:29.300 --> 01:45:33.270
feel free dabbling in the
second rate stuff, so to speak?

01:45:33.270 --> 01:45:36.290
And if that's not
the case, isn't there

01:45:36.290 --> 01:45:39.650
perhaps something wrong or
something inadequate right

01:45:39.650 --> 01:45:43.523
now with the entire concept
of a sovereign nation at all?

01:45:43.523 --> 01:45:44.940
MORRISON: Well,
to the last point,

01:45:44.940 --> 01:45:47.300
I think there probably is
quite a lot wrong with it.

01:45:47.300 --> 01:45:48.620
But I'm not in--

01:45:48.620 --> 01:45:50.930
I'm too old to engage
in the projection

01:45:50.930 --> 01:45:52.760
of large historical changes.

01:45:52.760 --> 01:45:55.340
They might come, but I don't
know when they'll come.

01:45:55.340 --> 01:45:57.440
But I do find it not at
all difficult to see.

01:45:57.440 --> 01:46:02.510
I mean, restraint is the
essence of contemporary war.

01:46:02.510 --> 01:46:03.110
We've seen it.

01:46:03.110 --> 01:46:06.470
We've fought a war in which
we were rather badly beaten.

01:46:06.470 --> 01:46:08.210
But we very rarely--

01:46:08.210 --> 01:46:10.880
even only considered
extending that war because

01:46:10.880 --> 01:46:12.890
of the obvious fact
that if we extended

01:46:12.890 --> 01:46:15.360
that war at the nuclear
direction and escalated it,

01:46:15.360 --> 01:46:17.540
we would've lost more
than we would have gained.

01:46:17.540 --> 01:46:20.090
And that option is available
at any point in any war.

01:46:20.090 --> 01:46:21.590
I look forward to
the possibility

01:46:21.590 --> 01:46:23.960
that that could happen, that
even the great states would

01:46:23.960 --> 01:46:26.087
confront each other
in war, recognizing

01:46:26.087 --> 01:46:27.920
that if they were to
open it to nuclear war,

01:46:27.920 --> 01:46:30.890
they would lose something
more than the issues at stake.

01:46:30.890 --> 01:46:32.840
We are happy that they
are not near neighbors.

01:46:32.840 --> 01:46:34.715
They don't have-- it's
not a question of they

01:46:34.715 --> 01:46:37.020
will take Washington tomorrow.

01:46:37.020 --> 01:46:39.290
The only way in which the
United States is endangered

01:46:39.290 --> 01:46:41.120
is through nuclear weapons--

01:46:41.120 --> 01:46:43.400
the zone of the interior.

01:46:43.400 --> 01:46:45.890
We've given up this great
geopolitical advantage

01:46:45.890 --> 01:46:48.383
for the sake of waging strategic
war far away, which is not

01:46:48.383 --> 01:46:49.550
a very sensible thing to do.

01:46:49.550 --> 01:46:52.640
But that's another talk
that I won't give now.

01:46:52.640 --> 01:46:56.090
LOW: But probably the
war, the total war

01:46:56.090 --> 01:46:59.360
with unconditional
surrender is out.

01:46:59.360 --> 01:47:00.230
That's finished.

01:47:00.230 --> 01:47:02.450
Because that would
probably lead to nuclear.

01:47:02.450 --> 01:47:04.860
MORRISON: Probably, yeah.

01:47:04.860 --> 01:47:07.310
LOW: Yes, sir--

01:47:07.310 --> 01:47:09.230
AUDIENCE: During
World War II, we

01:47:09.230 --> 01:47:11.990
had the Manhattan Project,
which was a research

01:47:11.990 --> 01:47:14.940
project, which people didn't
know what the results would be.

01:47:14.940 --> 01:47:17.480
But if they were
successful, would

01:47:17.480 --> 01:47:21.590
certainly, and in fact, we can
see from with our hindsight,

01:47:21.590 --> 01:47:25.460
certainly change the nature
of warfare and world politics.

01:47:25.460 --> 01:47:27.740
Today, in the United
States, we have

01:47:27.740 --> 01:47:29.903
Star Wars, or the Strategic
Defense Initiative.

01:47:29.903 --> 01:47:31.820
Depending on whether
you're for or against it,

01:47:31.820 --> 01:47:34.320
use one name or the other.

01:47:34.320 --> 01:47:38.750
Which again is, at the
moment, is a research program.

01:47:38.750 --> 01:47:47.400
Which if it was successful,
in total defense, if you will,

01:47:47.400 --> 01:47:50.490
would again change the nature
of warfare and world politics.

01:47:50.490 --> 01:47:52.650
And it seems to me that
some of the arguments

01:47:52.650 --> 01:47:56.730
for and against Star Wars
could have been applied back

01:47:56.730 --> 01:47:58.860
during World War II for
the Manhattan Project.

01:47:58.860 --> 01:48:00.330
Do you think that's valid?

01:48:00.330 --> 01:48:02.910
What similarities and
differences do you see?

01:48:02.910 --> 01:48:03.990
MORRISON: Well, it's an
extremely interesting,

01:48:03.990 --> 01:48:04.830
logical argument.

01:48:04.830 --> 01:48:08.370
And I certainly would like
to give you a large big red A

01:48:08.370 --> 01:48:10.580
for debating merit.

01:48:10.580 --> 01:48:12.310
I think it's extremely well put.

01:48:12.310 --> 01:48:14.268
But as a matter of fact,
I think you're selling

01:48:14.268 --> 01:48:16.053
this perpetual motion machine.

01:48:16.053 --> 01:48:17.720
I don't agree-- I
don't disagree at all.

01:48:17.720 --> 01:48:19.137
And if you would
explain to me how

01:48:19.137 --> 01:48:22.350
it's going to make
nuclear weapons, what

01:48:22.350 --> 01:48:25.530
is it-- impotent and
obsolete, I'll sign onto it.

01:48:25.530 --> 01:48:26.673
It just won't.

01:48:26.673 --> 01:48:27.840
And the reason it won't is--

01:48:27.840 --> 01:48:29.423
excuse me, I'm going
to make a remark.

01:48:29.423 --> 01:48:31.830
It's not even
logically coherent.

01:48:31.830 --> 01:48:34.680
What it really meant to
say was, logically, it'll

01:48:34.680 --> 01:48:37.260
make ballistic missiles
impotent and obsolete.

01:48:37.260 --> 01:48:38.760
That's the most it can claim.

01:48:38.760 --> 01:48:40.200
Maybe it'll add cruise missiles.

01:48:40.200 --> 01:48:41.950
But I'll be darned if
I see how it's going

01:48:41.950 --> 01:48:44.070
to work on submarine torpedoes.

01:48:44.070 --> 01:48:46.696
It isn't just discussing
the right issue.

01:48:46.696 --> 01:48:48.961
[APPLAUSE]

01:48:49.748 --> 01:48:51.540
So I personally think
it's a bill of goods.

01:48:51.540 --> 01:48:54.082
I mean, the difference between
that and the Manhattan Project

01:48:54.082 --> 01:48:56.030
was that when somebody
was willing enough

01:48:56.030 --> 01:48:58.540
to put in $10 million dollars
into it, in the OSRD, probably

01:48:58.540 --> 01:49:01.160
Kohnert and Bush,
and then it went.

01:49:01.160 --> 01:49:03.060
And then [INAUDIBLE]
made the chain reaction.

01:49:03.060 --> 01:49:04.700
And after that, it
looked pretty clear.

01:49:04.700 --> 01:49:07.100
Another $500 million,
and so it went.

01:49:07.100 --> 01:49:08.720
Well, that's what
has to be done here.

01:49:08.720 --> 01:49:10.428
By the way, the chips
are now much bigger

01:49:10.428 --> 01:49:11.766
for much less delivery.

01:49:16.230 --> 01:49:21.190
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
the emphasis here

01:49:21.190 --> 01:49:24.166
on the arms races itself.

01:49:24.166 --> 01:49:28.134
And, I guess I would
address this to both of you,

01:49:28.134 --> 01:49:31.110
as to why you are addressing
such overwhelming importance

01:49:31.110 --> 01:49:32.996
to the arms race.

01:49:32.996 --> 01:49:34.954
If one takes the point
of view, and I think one

01:49:34.954 --> 01:49:38.054
might, that the
risks of nuclear war

01:49:38.054 --> 01:49:41.030
are relatively independent
of either the size

01:49:41.030 --> 01:49:44.006
or potential characteristics
of the arsenal,

01:49:44.006 --> 01:49:49.462
then it's likely that even
if we add another 25,000

01:49:49.462 --> 01:49:55.414
warheads to the arsenal,
or [INAUDIBLE] about 50,000

01:49:55.414 --> 01:49:58.910
warheads now, the radical
[INAUDIBLE] proposal,

01:49:58.910 --> 01:50:04.800
my judgment would be that
the risks of nuclear war

01:50:04.800 --> 01:50:06.903
would not be
materially different,

01:50:06.903 --> 01:50:09.255
until you get to a very
reduced level, though.

01:50:09.255 --> 01:50:12.225
And that, in fact, in
the foreseeable future,

01:50:12.225 --> 01:50:17.175
the risk of nuclear war, we
have relatively much less

01:50:17.175 --> 01:50:20.640
to do with the size and
[INAUDIBLE] of the arsenal

01:50:20.640 --> 01:50:26.085
[INAUDIBLE] and much more to do
with the political conditions

01:50:26.085 --> 01:50:28.806
that govern relations
between super powers

01:50:28.806 --> 01:50:33.015
and between the super
powers and the third world.

01:50:33.015 --> 01:50:40.384
And I'm curious how you would
both judge the risks of war,

01:50:40.384 --> 01:50:44.360
the risk of nuclear war,
in terms of the arms race,

01:50:44.360 --> 01:50:46.348
per se, how important
you think the arms

01:50:46.348 --> 01:50:48.833
race is a conditioned risk.

01:50:48.833 --> 01:50:55.820
And second, why [INAUDIBLE]

01:50:55.820 --> 01:50:59.890
WEISSKOPF: Well, I'm sorry that
my talk wasn't good enough,

01:50:59.890 --> 01:51:06.470
because that's what I--
that's what I thought I said.

01:51:06.470 --> 01:51:09.260
I mean, the arms race,
I agree with you,

01:51:09.260 --> 01:51:12.440
with some kind of exaggeration.

01:51:12.440 --> 01:51:16.540
I would say, the arms
race is a symptom.

01:51:16.540 --> 01:51:22.020
I mean, if two superpowers are
really capable of producing

01:51:22.020 --> 01:51:26.460
and increasing armaments
that are 50 times larger

01:51:26.460 --> 01:51:30.210
than complete destruction,
something is wrong.

01:51:30.210 --> 01:51:33.540
I mean, this is the mental
disease I was talking about.

01:51:33.540 --> 01:51:36.990
And what I was trying to say,
maybe not clearly enough,

01:51:36.990 --> 01:51:39.750
was that you need a
change of attitude.

01:51:39.750 --> 01:51:44.170
That the arms-- not only you
cannot turn out the arms race,

01:51:44.170 --> 01:51:47.700
but you will get a stable
instead of meta stable

01:51:47.700 --> 01:51:51.510
situation, because the arms
race is a meta stable situation.

01:51:51.510 --> 01:51:56.070
Only if you change the
attitude towards our adversary

01:51:56.070 --> 01:51:57.670
in both directions.

01:51:57.670 --> 01:52:00.820
And if you mean
that, I fully agree.

01:52:00.820 --> 01:52:02.960
And I thought I said it.

01:52:02.960 --> 01:52:05.447
But maybe not clear enough.

01:52:05.447 --> 01:52:07.780
MORRISON: Well, maybe there
is a difference between Viki

01:52:07.780 --> 01:52:09.280
and me, because I
really don't think

01:52:09.280 --> 01:52:10.897
I agree with what you say.

01:52:10.897 --> 01:52:12.730
I really think it's not
the risk of war that

01:52:12.730 --> 01:52:13.990
counts, but the expectation.

01:52:17.790 --> 01:52:19.930
And the expectation
is very high,

01:52:19.930 --> 01:52:22.540
even if the risk is small.

01:52:22.540 --> 01:52:25.590
Because the damage is so great
if it occurs in the wrong way.

01:52:25.590 --> 01:52:27.190
And it's against
this expectation

01:52:27.190 --> 01:52:29.260
that I'm primarily acting.

01:52:29.260 --> 01:52:32.420
I don't expect to
see an end to war.

01:52:32.420 --> 01:52:34.330
It might be, as he
says, a total war

01:52:34.330 --> 01:52:36.310
with unconditional
surrender demands,

01:52:36.310 --> 01:52:38.200
but that's in the
political domain.

01:52:38.200 --> 01:52:41.440
What I mean is war
with the maximum use

01:52:41.440 --> 01:52:46.360
of the technical capabilities
of modern society.

01:52:46.360 --> 01:52:48.280
I don't think that
we could stand that.

01:52:48.280 --> 01:52:49.720
And I think that the only--

01:52:49.720 --> 01:52:52.210
way to get around
it is to remove

01:52:52.210 --> 01:52:55.960
the serious consequences,
as far as possible,

01:52:55.960 --> 01:52:59.040
by reducing nuclear armaments,
and their triggerness,

01:52:59.040 --> 01:53:03.580
their readiness, their
instability, their first strike

01:53:03.580 --> 01:53:06.040
advantages, all
these issues that

01:53:06.040 --> 01:53:09.946
have come up in the theology
of the nuclear doctrine.

01:53:09.946 --> 01:53:12.898
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the size
of the arsenal and [INAUDIBLE]

01:53:12.898 --> 01:53:15.850
confrontation
between super powers.

01:53:15.850 --> 01:53:17.326
The question
whether super powers

01:53:17.326 --> 01:53:25.362
will escalate toward a nuclear
war, [INAUDIBLE] The question

01:53:25.362 --> 01:53:28.110
of whether or not
they will escalate,

01:53:28.110 --> 01:53:30.680
which I gather you're saying
is possible without [INAUDIBLE]

01:53:30.680 --> 01:53:32.190
MORRISON: Yes.

01:53:32.190 --> 01:53:35.840
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the
prospects of that [INAUDIBLE]

01:53:35.840 --> 01:53:39.720
are relative as to the size
of [INAUDIBLE] the arsenal

01:53:39.720 --> 01:53:44.765
and much more related to the
political conditions that

01:53:44.765 --> 01:53:48.887
govern relations [INAUDIBLE]
and the political rules

01:53:48.887 --> 01:53:56.475
of conduct that have
developed [INAUDIBLE]

01:53:56.475 --> 01:53:58.100
MORRISON: I just
don't agree with that.

01:53:58.100 --> 01:53:59.030
I think you're wrong.

01:53:59.030 --> 01:54:01.480
I think that the concrete
circumstances, what

01:54:01.480 --> 01:54:03.310
are called the
capabilities, are more

01:54:03.310 --> 01:54:05.020
determining than
judgments of intentions

01:54:05.020 --> 01:54:06.280
and rules of engagement.

01:54:06.280 --> 01:54:08.050
That's characteristic
of most war.

01:54:08.050 --> 01:54:09.330
And I think if you
cut this thing down--

01:54:09.330 --> 01:54:10.870
I agree a factor of two won't
make a bit of difference

01:54:10.870 --> 01:54:12.140
from my point of view.

01:54:12.140 --> 01:54:13.777
But it only shows direction.

01:54:13.777 --> 01:54:15.610
Therefore, any small
change is valuable now,

01:54:15.610 --> 01:54:17.200
because it signals direction.

01:54:17.200 --> 01:54:19.520
But if you cut it by
an order of magnitude,

01:54:19.520 --> 01:54:21.720
which is what I'm
speaking of, then I

01:54:21.720 --> 01:54:23.053
think it makes a big difference.

01:54:23.053 --> 01:54:24.963
It no longer is decisive
in and of itself.

01:54:24.963 --> 01:54:27.130
It no longer represents
anything but another gamble,

01:54:27.130 --> 01:54:29.590
if you try to escalate now,
who will make the most weapons,

01:54:29.590 --> 01:54:31.882
and who will do them first,
and who will end them best.

01:54:31.882 --> 01:54:34.210
That's putting the whole
war on a certain gamble

01:54:34.210 --> 01:54:36.940
of novel technology,
which is very hard to do,

01:54:36.940 --> 01:54:39.620
which I don't anticipate
people would manage.

01:54:39.620 --> 01:54:42.640
So I think that it is
possible, without increasing

01:54:42.640 --> 01:54:45.610
the risk of major
superpower conflict,

01:54:45.610 --> 01:54:47.740
to reduce the number of
weapons, and very greatly

01:54:47.740 --> 01:54:50.930
reduce the instability
of the situation.

01:54:50.930 --> 01:54:53.080
LOW: I'll take just
one more question.

01:54:53.080 --> 01:54:54.280
I want to end at 10.

01:54:54.280 --> 01:54:56.746
Yes, sir--

01:54:56.746 --> 01:55:00.730
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
that there's

01:55:00.730 --> 01:55:03.580
a moral distinction between
killing a million people

01:55:03.580 --> 01:55:06.280
and killing 4 billion
people, [INAUDIBLE]

01:55:06.280 --> 01:55:08.560
Conventional warfare's OK.

01:55:08.560 --> 01:55:10.610
MORRISON: No.

01:55:10.610 --> 01:55:11.350
I don't--

01:55:11.350 --> 01:55:12.938
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:55:12.938 --> 01:55:14.980
MORRISON: No, but there's
a practical distinction

01:55:14.980 --> 01:55:17.740
of great importance.

01:55:17.740 --> 01:55:21.597
Namely important to
3,999,000,000 people,

01:55:21.597 --> 01:55:22.555
if I take your numbers.

01:55:25.140 --> 01:55:27.120
Look, we've seen 20
million people killed,

01:55:27.120 --> 01:55:30.180
roughly speaking, in wars since
World War II, maybe only 15.

01:55:30.180 --> 01:55:32.347
AUDIENCE: And there's nothing
wrong with [INAUDIBLE]

01:55:32.347 --> 01:55:33.713
MORRISON: No, that's terrible.

01:55:33.713 --> 01:55:35.130
But do you think
that the presence

01:55:35.130 --> 01:55:37.950
of nuclear power,
superpower armaments

01:55:37.950 --> 01:55:40.227
has made that less likely?

01:55:40.227 --> 01:55:42.060
I think it's contributed
a great deal to it.

01:55:42.060 --> 01:55:44.730
Client states everywhere have
been put to work warring,

01:55:44.730 --> 01:55:48.424
because the big boys didn't want
them engage in it themselves.

01:55:48.424 --> 01:55:49.882
I feel that's the
principal reason.

01:55:55.398 --> 01:55:56.440
LOW: Thank you very much.

01:55:56.440 --> 01:55:57.640
Thank you for coming.

01:55:57.640 --> 01:55:58.780
Thank you.

01:55:58.780 --> 01:56:01.530
[APPLAUSE]

